window.WB_BOOK=window.WB_BOOK||{};window.WB_BOOK["week03"]={"id":"week03","n":3,"section":"A","label":"","title":"Motion & Newton's Laws","titleText":"Motion & Newton's Laws","subtitle":"How things move, why they move, and what happens when they collide","intro":"Last week you learned what forces are. This week you learn what forces DO: they change how things move. You will learn the five words that describe motion (distance, displacement, speed, velocity, acceleration), how to read motion graphs, Newton's three laws, the equation ⟪F=ma⟫, and momentum. These topics appear on almost every Paper 01 and Paper 02, and the graph questions are some of the easiest marks in the exam once you know two rules: GRADIENT and AREA.","objectives":[{"code":"A4.1","text":"Define distance, displacement, speed, velocity and acceleration"},{"code":"A4.2","text":"Use displacement–time and velocity–time graphs (gradient and area)"},{"code":"A4.3","text":"Discuss Aristotle's arguments about motion and why they were replaced"},{"code":"A4.4","text":"State Newton's three laws of motion"},{"code":"A4.5","text":"Use Newton's laws (including ⟪F=ma⟫) to explain and calculate motion in dynamic systems"},{"code":"A4.6","text":"Define linear momentum and use ⟪p=mv⟫"},{"code":"A4.7","text":"Identify situations in which linear momentum is conserved (collisions, explosions, recoil, rockets)"},{"code":"A4.8","text":"Apply the law of conservation of linear momentum to solve problems"}],"formulaCard":[{"text":"average speed = total distance ÷ total time","meaning":"scalar","units":"m s^−1^"},{"text":"velocity = displacement ÷ time","meaning":"vector: speed in a stated direction","units":"m s^−1^"},{"tex":"a=(v-u)/t;\\quad v=u+\\text{ at}","meaning":"u = initial velocity, v = final velocity, t = time","units":"m s^−2^"},{"tex":"\\mathrm{km}\\,\\mathrm{h}^{-1}\\div 3.6=\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}","meaning":"unit conversion (× 3.6 to go back)","units":""},{"tex":"\\text{gradient of }s\\text{–}t\\text{ graph }=\\text{ velocity}","meaning":"steeper = faster; tangent for a curve","units":"m s^−1^"},{"tex":"\\text{gradient of }v\\text{–}t\\text{ graph }=\\text{ acceleration}","meaning":"negative gradient = deceleration","units":"m s^−2^"},{"tex":"\\text{area under }v\\text{–}t\\text{ graph }=\\text{ displacement}","meaning":"split into rectangles + triangles","units":"m"},{"tex":"F=ma","meaning":"F = RESULTANT force; ⟪1\\,\\mathrm{N}=1\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫","units":"N"},{"tex":"W=mg","meaning":"⟪g=10\\,\\mathrm{N}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}⟫","units":"N"},{"tex":"p=mv","meaning":"momentum (vector)","units":"⟪\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}=\\mathrm{N}\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫"},{"tex":"F=(mv-mu)/t;\\quad Ft=\\Delta p","meaning":"force = rate of change of momentum; Ft = impulse","units":"N; N s"},{"tex":"m_{1}u_{1}+m_{2}u_{2}=m_{1}v_{1}+m_{2}v_{2}","meaning":"conservation of momentum (no external force)","units":"kg m s^−1^"},{"tex":"\\text{Stick together: }m_{1}u_{1}+m_{2}u_{2}=(m_{1}+m_{2})v","meaning":"common velocity after collision","units":"m s^−1^"},{"text":"Terminal velocity: drag = weight","meaning":"resultant ⟪=0⟫, ⟪a=0⟫","units":""}],"glossary":[{"term":"Distance","def":"Total length of path travelled (scalar)."},{"term":"Displacement","def":"Distance moved in a stated direction from the starting point (vector)."},{"term":"Speed","def":"Rate of change of distance (scalar)."},{"term":"Velocity","def":"Rate of change of displacement; speed in a stated direction (vector)."},{"term":"Acceleration","def":"Rate of change of velocity (vector), m s^−2^."},{"term":"Deceleration","def":"Negative acceleration — slowing down."},{"term":"Uniform","def":"Constant, not changing (e.g. uniform acceleration)."},{"term":"Gradient","def":"Steepness of a graph ⟪=\\Delta y\\div \\Delta x⟫."},{"term":"Tangent","def":"Straight line touching a curve at one point; its gradient gives the rate at that point."},{"term":"Resultant force","def":"The single force with the same effect as all the forces acting together."},{"term":"Inertia","def":"Reluctance of a body to change its state of rest or motion; depends on mass."},{"term":"Newton (N)","def":"Force that gives 1 kg an acceleration of 1 m s^−2^."},{"term":"Friction","def":"Force opposing motion between surfaces in contact."},{"term":"Terminal velocity","def":"Constant velocity of a falling body when drag equals weight."},{"term":"Free-body diagram","def":"Sketch of one body showing every force acting on it as an arrow."},{"term":"Momentum","def":"Mass × velocity (vector), kg m s^−1^."},{"term":"Impulse","def":"Force × time = change in momentum, N s."},{"term":"Closed (isolated) system","def":"A group of bodies on which no external resultant force acts."},{"term":"Recoil","def":"Backward motion of a gun (or other body) when it pushes something forwards."},{"term":"Friction-compensated runway","def":"Runway tilted just enough that a trolley rolls at constant speed, so friction is balanced."}],"lessons":[{"id":"week03.3.1","kind":"unit","unit":"3.1","title":"Describing Motion: Distance, Displacement, Speed, Velocity, Acceleration","titleText":"Describing Motion: Distance, Displacement, Speed, Velocity, Acceleration","goals":[{"code":"A4.1","text":"Define distance, displacement, speed, velocity and acceleration"}],"codes":["A4.1"],"words":[{"term":"Distance","def":"Total length of path travelled (scalar)."},{"term":"Displacement","def":"Distance moved in a stated direction from the starting point (vector)."},{"term":"Speed","def":"Rate of change of distance (scalar)."},{"term":"Velocity","def":"Rate of change of displacement; speed in a stated direction (vector)."},{"term":"Acceleration","def":"Rate of change of velocity (vector), m s^−2^."},{"term":"Deceleration","def":"Negative acceleration — slowing down."},{"term":"Uniform","def":"Constant, not changing (e.g. uniform acceleration)."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"To describe how something moves, Physics uses five key quantities. Two of them are about **how far**, two are about **how fast**, and one is about **how quickly the velocity changes**."},{"type":"h","text":"How far: distance and displacement"},{"type":"def","term":"Distance","text":"the total length of the path travelled, in any direction. It is a **scalar**. Unit: metre (m)."},{"type":"def","term":"Displacement","text":"the distance moved in a **stated direction** — the straight-line distance from the starting point to the finishing point, with its direction. It is a **vector**. Unit: metre (m)."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week03/dist_disp","alt":"Figure 3.1 — Walk 400 m east then 300 m north: distance = 700 m, but displacement = 500 m in direction θ north of east","caption":"Figure 3.1 — Walk 400 m east then 300 m north: distance ⟪=700\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫, but displacement ⟪=500\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ in direction θ north of east","width":3.6,"w":894,"h":600},{"type":"remember","text":"If you finish where you started (e.g. one full lap of a running track), your displacement is **zero**, even though the distance may be 400 m."},{"type":"h","text":"How fast: speed and velocity"},{"type":"def","term":"Speed","text":"the distance travelled per unit time (the rate of change of distance). Scalar. Unit: m s^−1^."},{"type":"def","term":"Velocity","text":"the displacement per unit time (the rate of change of displacement), i.e. speed in a stated direction. Vector. Unit: m s^−1^."},{"type":"formula","text":"average speed = total distance ÷ total time        average velocity = displacement ÷ time","where":["Distance and displacement in metres (m), time in seconds (s), speed and velocity in m s^−1^.","Two cars both moving at 20 m s^−1^ have the same speed; if one goes north and the other south their velocities are different (+20 and ⟪-20\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫)."],"label":"SPEED AND VELOCITY"},{"type":"tip","text":"To change **km h^−1^ to m s^−1^ divide by 3.6**. To change **m s^−1^ to km h^−1^ multiply by 3.6**. Why? ⟪1\\,\\mathrm{km}\\,\\mathrm{h}^{-1}=1000\\,\\mathrm{m}\\div 3600\\,\\mathrm{s}=1/3.6\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫. Example: ⟪72\\,\\mathrm{km}\\,\\mathrm{h}^{-1}\\div 3.6=20\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫.","label":"UNIT CONVERSION"},{"type":"h","text":"Changing velocity: acceleration"},{"type":"def","term":"Acceleration","text":"the rate of change of velocity, i.e. the change in velocity per unit time. Vector. Unit: m s^−2^."},{"type":"formula","tex":"a=(v-u)/t\\text{ which rearranges to }v=u+\\text{ at}","where":["⟪a⟫ = acceleration (m s^−2^); u = initial (starting) velocity (m s^−1^); v = final velocity (m s^−1^); t = time taken (s)","**Uniform** (constant) acceleration means the velocity changes by the same amount every second."],"label":"ACCELERATION"},{"type":"p","text":"An acceleration of 3 m s^−2^ means the velocity **increases by 3 m s^−1^ every second**. If a body is slowing down, v is smaller than u, so a comes out **negative**. A negative acceleration is called a **deceleration** (or retardation)."},{"type":"warn","text":"Do not mix up the units. Speed and velocity: **m s^−1^** (metres per second). Acceleration: **m s^−2^** (metres per second, per second). Writing m/s for an acceleration loses the unit mark."},{"type":"p","text":"**Velocity can change in two ways**: its size (speed up or slow down) OR its direction. A car going round a roundabout at a steady 10 m s^−1^ is accelerating, because the **direction** of its velocity keeps changing."},{"type":"h","text":"Summary table"},{"type":"table","headers":["Quantity","Scalar or vector?","Formula","Unit"],"rows":[["Distance","scalar","—","m"],["Displacement","vector","—","m"],["Speed","scalar","distance ÷ time","m s^−1^"],["Velocity","vector","displacement ÷ time","m s^−1^"],["Acceleration","vector","⟪(v-u)\\div t⟫","m s^−2^"]]},{"type":"tip","text":"In a \"define\" question, use the words **\"rate of change of\"** or **\"per unit time\"**. \"Acceleration is how fast you go\" scores ZERO. \"Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity\" scores the mark."}],"examples":[{"id":"week03.3.1.e1","title":"Distance vs displacement","question":"A student walks 400 m due east and then 300 m due north. The whole walk takes 500 s. Find (a) the distance travelled, (b) the displacement, (c) the average speed, (d) the average velocity.","img":{"src":"img/week03/dist_disp","alt":"A student walks 400 m due east and then 300 m due north. The whole walk takes 500 s. Find (a) the distance travelled, (b) the displacement, (c) the average speed, (d) the average velocity.","w":894,"h":600},"steps":[{"label":"(a) Distance","text":"add the lengths of the path: ⟪400+300=\\boldsymbol{700\\,\\mathrm{m}}⟫"},{"label":"(b) Displacement — size","text":"straight line from start to finish (Pythagoras): ⟪\\sqrt{400^{2}+300^{2}}=\\sqrt{250\\,000}=\\boldsymbol{500\\,\\mathrm{m}}⟫"},{"label":"(b) Displacement — direction","text":"⟪\\tan \\theta =300\\div 400=0.75⟫, so ⟪\\theta =\\tan ^{-1}(0.75)=\\boldsymbol{36.9^{\\circ}}⟫** north of east**"},{"label":"(c) Average speed","text":"= distance ÷ time ⟪=700\\div 500=\\boldsymbol{1.4\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}}⟫"},{"label":"(d) Average velocity","text":"= displacement ÷ time ⟪=500\\div 500=\\boldsymbol{1.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}}⟫** at 36.9° north of east**"}],"answer":"700 m; 500 m at 36.9° N of E; 1.4 m s^−1^; 1.0 m s^−1^ at 36.9° N of E"},{"id":"week03.3.1.e2","title":"Converting km h⁻¹ and using speed = distance ÷ time","question":"A minibus travels at a steady 72 km h^−1^. (a) Convert this to m s^−1^. (b) How long does it take to travel 1.5 km?","steps":[{"label":"(a) Convert","text":"⟪72\\div 3.6=\\boldsymbol{20\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}}⟫"},{"label":"(b) Formula","text":"speed = distance ÷ time, so time = distance ÷ speed"},{"label":"Convert units first","text":"⟪1.5\\,\\mathrm{km}=1500\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪t=1500\\div 20⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪t=\\boldsymbol{75\\,\\mathrm{s}}⟫"}],"answer":"20 m s^−1^; 75 s"},{"id":"week03.3.1.e3","title":"Acceleration","question":"A car increases its velocity from 5.0 m s^−1^ to 25 m s^−1^ in 8.0 s. Calculate its acceleration.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪a=(v-u)/t⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪a=(25-5.0)/8.0=20/8.0⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪a=\\boldsymbol{2.5\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}}⟫"}],"answer":"2.5 m s^−2^"},{"id":"week03.3.1.e4","title":"Deceleration (negative acceleration)","question":"A bus moving at 30 m s^−1^ brakes and stops in 6.0 s. Calculate its acceleration and state its deceleration.","steps":[{"label":"Formula","text":"a = (v − u) / t   (v = 0 because it stops)"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪a=(0-30)/6.0=-30/6.0⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪a=\\boldsymbol{-5.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}}⟫. The minus sign means it is slowing down: deceleration ⟪=\\boldsymbol{5.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}}⟫."}],"answer":"⟪a=-5.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫ (deceleration of 5.0 m s^−2^)"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week03.3.1.p1","kind":"self","q":"Distinguish between **distance** and **displacement**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Distance is the total length of path travelled; it is a scalar (1). Displacement is the straight-line distance from start to finish in a stated direction; it is a vector (1).","points":[{"text":"Distance is the total length of path travelled; it is a scalar","marks":1},{"text":"Displacement is the straight-line distance from start to finish in a stated direction; it is a vector","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Distance vs displacement). Start like this: (a) Distance: add the lengths of the path: ⟪400+300=\\boldsymbol{700\\,\\mathrm{m}}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week03.3.1.p2","kind":"self","q":"Sort these into scalars and vectors: distance, velocity, speed, acceleration, displacement, time.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Scalars: distance, speed, time.\nVectors: velocity, acceleration, displacement.\n(1 mark for every two correctly placed, max 3.)","points":[{"text":"Scalars: distance, speed, time.\nVectors: velocity, acceleration, displacement.\n(1 mark for every two correctly placed, max 3.)","marks":3}],"conf":"low","section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Acceleration). Start like this: Formula: ⟪a=(v-u)/t⟫","from":3}}],"practice":[{"id":"week03.3.1.p3","kind":"self","q":"(a) Convert 90 km h^−1^ to m s^−1^.  (b) Convert 15 m s^−1^ to km h^−1^.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(a) ⟪90\\div 3.6=25\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).  (b) ⟪15\\times 3.6=54\\,\\mathrm{km}\\,\\mathrm{h}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) ⟪90\\div 3.6=25\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"(b) ⟪15\\times 3.6=54\\,\\mathrm{km}\\,\\mathrm{h}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Converting km h⁻¹ and using speed = distance ÷ time). Start like this: (a) Convert: ⟪72\\div 3.6=\\boldsymbol{20\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}}⟫","from":2}},{"id":"week03.3.1.p4","kind":"num","q":"A sprinter runs 100 m in 12.5 s. Calculate her average speed.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"average speed = distance ÷ time ⟪=100\\div 12.5⟫ (1) ⟪=8.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"average speed = distance ÷ time ⟪=100\\div 12.5⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=8.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":8,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Distance vs displacement). Start like this: (a) Distance: add the lengths of the path: ⟪400+300=\\boldsymbol{700\\,\\mathrm{m}}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week03.3.1.p5","kind":"self","q":"An athlete runs exactly one lap of a 400 m track in 80 s and finishes where she started. State (a) the distance, (b) the displacement, and calculate (c) her average speed, (d) her average velocity.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"(a) 400 m (1). (b) 0 m — she ends at the start (1). (c) ⟪400\\div 80=5.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1). (d) ⟪0\\div 80=0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) 400 m","marks":1},{"text":"(b) 0 m — she ends at the start","marks":1},{"text":"(c) ⟪400\\div 80=5.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"(d) ⟪0\\div 80=0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Acceleration). Start like this: Formula: ⟪a=(v-u)/t⟫","from":3}},{"id":"week03.3.1.p6","kind":"num","q":"A car accelerates uniformly from rest to 27 m s^−1^ in 9.0 s. Calculate its acceleration.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪a=(v-u)/t=(27-0)/9.0⟫ (1) ⟪=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪a=(v-u)/t=(27-0)/9.0⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":3,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−2^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Acceleration). Start like this: Formula: ⟪a=(v-u)/t⟫","from":3}},{"id":"week03.3.1.p7","kind":"num","q":"A cyclist moving at 6.0 m s^−1^ accelerates at 1.5 m s^−2^ for 4.0 s. Calculate her final velocity.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪v=u⟫ + at (1) ⟪=6.0+(1.5\\times 4.0)⟫ (1) ⟪=6.0+6.0=12\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪v=u⟫ + at","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=6.0+(1.5\\times 4.0)⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=6.0+6.0=12\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":12,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Acceleration). Start like this: Formula: ⟪a=(v-u)/t⟫","from":3}},{"id":"week03.3.1.p8","kind":"num","q":"A truck travelling at 20 m s^−1^ brakes with a deceleration of 4.0 m s^−2^. How long does it take to stop?","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪a=-4.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫, ⟪v=0⟫. ⟪t=(v-u)/a⟫ (1) ⟪=(0-20)/(-4.0)⟫ (1) ⟪=5.0\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪a=-4.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫, ⟪v=0⟫. ⟪t=(v-u)/a⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=(0-20)/(-4.0)⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=5.0\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":5,"sf":2,"unit":"s","unitTex":"\\mathrm{s}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Converting km h⁻¹ and using speed = distance ÷ time). Start like this: (a) Convert: ⟪72\\div 3.6=\\boldsymbol{20\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}}⟫","from":2}}],"exit":[{"id":"week03.3.1.p9","kind":"self","q":"A car goes round a roundabout at a constant speed of 10 m s^−1^. Explain why the car is accelerating.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Its direction is changing, so its velocity is changing (velocity is a vector) (1). Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, so it is accelerating even though its speed is constant (1).","points":[{"text":"Its direction is changing, so its velocity is changing (velocity is a vector)","marks":1},{"text":"Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity, so it is accelerating even though its speed is constant","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week03.3.1.p10","kind":"self","q":"A ball is thrown straight up at 15 m s^−1^. It reaches the top of its flight (momentarily at rest) after 1.5 s. Calculate its acceleration and explain the meaning of the sign of your answer.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪a=(0-15)/1.5⟫ (1) ⟪=-10\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫ (1). The negative sign means the acceleration is opposite to the (upward) motion — the ball is slowing down / the acceleration is downward (due to gravity) (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪a=(0-15)/1.5⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=-10\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"The negative sign means the acceleration is opposite to the (upward) motion — the ball is slowing down / the acceleration is downward (due to gravity)","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week03.3.1.w1","kind":"num","q":"Two forces of 5 N and 12 N act at right angles. What is the resultant?","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"⟪\\sqrt{25+144}=13\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫.","points":[{"text":"⟪\\sqrt{25+144}=13\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫.","marks":1}],"num":{"value":13,"sf":2,"unit":"N","unitTex":"\\mathrm{N}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week02.r1"},{"id":"week03.3.1.w2","kind":"self","q":"Write the newton in SI base units.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"kg m s⁻².","points":[{"text":"kg m s⁻².","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week02.r2"},{"id":"week03.3.1.w3","kind":"self","q":"State the principle of moments.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"In equilibrium, sum of clockwise moments = sum of anticlockwise moments about any point.","points":[{"text":"In equilibrium, sum of clockwise moments = sum of anticlockwise moments about any point.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week02.r3"},{"id":"week03.3.1.w4","kind":"self","q":"Give TWO ways to make an object more stable.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"Lower its centre of gravity; widen its base.","points":[{"text":"Lower its centre of gravity; widen its base.","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week02.r4"},{"id":"week03.3.1.w5","kind":"num","q":"A spring of ⟪k=200\\,\\mathrm{N}\\,\\mathrm{m}^{-1}⟫ carries 4.0 N. Find the extension.","marks":1,"level":1,"scheme":"⟪x=4.0/200=0.020\\,\\mathrm{m}=2.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫.","points":[{"text":"⟪x=4.0/200=0.020\\,\\mathrm{m}=2.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}⟫.","marks":1}],"num":{"value":2,"sf":2,"unit":"cm","unitTex":"\\mathrm{cm}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week02.r5"}],"quickStart":[{"type":"p","text":"You need four things from earlier weeks. Check each one before you start Unit 3.1."},{"type":"h","text":"1. Scalars and vectors (Week 2)"},{"type":"p","text":"A **scalar** has size (magnitude) only, e.g. mass, time, distance. A **vector** has size AND direction, e.g. force, weight, displacement. For vectors in a straight line we use **+ for one direction and − for the opposite direction**."},{"type":"h","text":"2. Resultant of forces in a straight line (Week 2)"},{"type":"p","text":"Same direction → ADD. Opposite directions → SUBTRACT, and the resultant acts in the direction of the bigger force. Example: 50 N right and 20 N left → resultant ⟪=30\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ to the right."},{"type":"h","text":"3. Weight ⟪W=mg⟫ (Week 2)"},{"type":"p","text":"Weight (N) = mass ⟪(\\mathrm{kg})\\times g⟫, with ⟪g=10\\,\\mathrm{N}\\,\\mathrm{kg}^{-1}⟫. A 60 kg person weighs ⟪60\\times 10=600\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫."},{"type":"h","text":"4. Areas of shapes (you need these for velocity–time graphs)"},{"type":"table","headers":["Shape","Area","Example"],"rows":[["Rectangle","base × height","⟪5\\times 4=20⟫"],["Triangle","½ × base × height","⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 6\\times 4=12⟫"],["Trapezium","½ × (sum of parallel sides) × width","⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times (3+5)\\times 2=8⟫"]]},{"type":"fill","text":"Try it: make **u** the subject of ⟪v=u⟫ + at.   u = ","lines":1},{"type":"fill","text":"Try it: make **t** the subject of ⟪a=(v-u)/t⟫.   t = ","lines":1}],"n":15},{"id":"week03.3.2","kind":"unit","unit":"3.2","title":"Motion Graphs: Displacement–Time and Velocity–Time","titleText":"Motion Graphs: Displacement–Time and Velocity–Time","goals":[{"code":"A4.2","text":"Use displacement–time and velocity–time graphs (gradient and area)"}],"codes":["A4.2"],"words":[{"term":"Distance","def":"Total length of path travelled (scalar)."},{"term":"Displacement","def":"Distance moved in a stated direction from the starting point (vector)."},{"term":"Speed","def":"Rate of change of distance (scalar)."},{"term":"Velocity","def":"Rate of change of displacement; speed in a stated direction (vector)."},{"term":"Acceleration","def":"Rate of change of velocity (vector), m s^−2^."},{"term":"Deceleration","def":"Negative acceleration — slowing down."},{"term":"Uniform","def":"Constant, not changing (e.g. uniform acceleration)."},{"term":"Gradient","def":"Steepness of a graph ⟪=\\Delta y\\div \\Delta x⟫."},{"term":"Tangent","def":"Straight line touching a curve at one point; its gradient gives the rate at that point."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"Graphs are the quickest way to describe a journey. Learn the **two golden rules** and you can answer almost every graph question."},{"type":"formula","tex":"s\\text{–}t\\text{ gradient }=v\\qquad v\\text{–}t\\text{ gradient }=a\\qquad v\\text{–}t\\text{ area }=\\text{ distance}","where":["**Rule 1:** the GRADIENT of a displacement–time graph = VELOCITY.","**Rule 2:** the GRADIENT of a velocity–time graph = ACCELERATION.","**Rule 3:** the AREA under a velocity–time graph = DISPLACEMENT (distance travelled).","Gradient ⟪=\\Delta y\\div \\Delta x⟫ = rise ÷ run (Week 1). Use a large triangle.","Area: split the region under the line into rectangles and triangles, find each area, then add."],"label":"THE GOLDEN RULES"},{"type":"h","text":"Displacement–time (s–t) graphs"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week03/st_shapes","alt":"Figure 3.2 — Shapes of displacement–time graphs","caption":"Figure 3.2 — Shapes of displacement–time graphs","width":6,"w":1400,"h":386},{"type":"bullets","items":["**Horizontal line** → displacement not changing → the object is **at rest** (velocity ⟪=0⟫).","**Straight sloping line** → constant gradient → **constant velocity**. Steeper line = faster.","**Curve getting steeper** → gradient increasing → velocity increasing → **accelerating**.","**Line sloping down** → negative gradient → moving **back towards the start**.","To find the velocity at one instant on a **curve**, draw a **tangent** at that point and find its gradient."]},{"type":"h","text":"Velocity–time (v–t) graphs"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week03/vt_shapes","alt":"Figure 3.3 — Shapes of velocity–time graphs","caption":"Figure 3.3 — Shapes of velocity–time graphs","width":6,"w":1400,"h":386},{"type":"bullets","items":["**Horizontal line** → velocity constant → acceleration ⟪=0⟫ (NOT at rest, unless the line is on the time axis).","**Straight line sloping up** → **uniform acceleration**.","**Straight line sloping down** → **uniform deceleration**.","**Curve that levels off** → acceleration decreasing to zero → object reaches a steady (terminal) velocity.","The **area** between the line and the time axis = distance travelled."]},{"type":"warn","text":"The most common mistake: a horizontal line on a v–t graph does NOT mean \"stopped\". It means **constant velocity**. Always read the axis label first — is it displacement or velocity?"},{"type":"h","text":"Method: answering a v–t graph question"},{"type":"steps","items":["Read the axis labels and units. Write down the coordinates of every corner of the graph.","Acceleration in a stage = (change in velocity) ÷ (time for that stage).","Distance = area. Label each shape (A, B, C…), find each area, then add them.","Average speed = TOTAL distance ÷ TOTAL time (NOT the average of the speeds).","Give units: m s^−2^ for acceleration, m for distance, m s^−1^ for speed."]},{"type":"h","text":"Measuring speed and acceleration in the lab: the ticker-timer"},{"type":"p","text":"A **ticker-timer** makes **50 dots every second** on a paper tape pulled by a moving trolley. So the time between two neighbouring dots is ⟪1/50=\\boldsymbol{0.02\\,\\mathrm{s}}⟫. A strip of **5 spaces** therefore takes ⟪5\\times 0.02=\\boldsymbol{0.10\\,\\mathrm{s}}⟫."},{"type":"img","src":"img/week03/ticker_tape","alt":"Figure 3.4 — Ticker tapes: equal gaps mean constant speed; growing gaps mean acceleration","caption":"Figure 3.4 — Ticker tapes: equal gaps mean constant speed; growing gaps mean acceleration","width":5.6,"w":1254,"h":219},{"type":"steps","items":["Speed = length of a strip ÷ time for that strip (e.g. 5-space strip → divide by 0.10 s).","Acceleration = (speed from a later strip − speed from an earlier strip) ÷ time between the middles of the two strips.","Light gates connected to a data-logger (or a stopwatch with marked distances) can also be used."]},{"type":"tip","text":"Exam questions often say \"Describe the motion\" for each stage of a graph. Give TWO things: the type of motion (accelerating / constant velocity / decelerating / at rest) AND the numbers (e.g. \"accelerates uniformly from 0 to 20 m s^−1^ in 10 s\")."}],"examples":[{"id":"week03.3.2.e1","title":"Reading a velocity–time graph","question":"The graph shows the motion of a car. Calculate (a) the acceleration in stage A, (b) the deceleration in stage C, (c) the total distance travelled, (d) the average speed for the whole journey.","img":{"src":"img/week03/vt_car","alt":"The graph shows the motion of a car. Calculate (a) the acceleration in stage A, (b) the deceleration in stage C, (c) the total distance travelled, (d) the average speed for the whole journey.","w":1092,"h":654},"steps":[{"label":"(a) Stage A","text":"⟪a=(v-u)/t=(20-0)\\div 10=\\boldsymbol{2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}}⟫"},{"label":"(b) Stage C","text":"⟪a=(0-20)\\div (35-30)=-20\\div 5=-4.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫, so deceleration ⟪=\\boldsymbol{4.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}}⟫"},{"label":"(c) Area A (triangle)","text":"⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 10\\times 20=100\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"},{"label":"Area B (rectangle)","text":"⟪(30-10)\\times 20=20\\times 20=400\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"},{"label":"Area C (triangle)","text":"⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 5\\times 20=50\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫"},{"label":"Total distance","text":"⟪100+400+50=\\boldsymbol{550\\,\\mathrm{m}}⟫"},{"label":"(d) Average speed","text":"total distance ÷ total time ⟪=550\\div 35=15.71⟫… ≈ **15.7 m s^−1^**"}],"answer":"2.0 m s^−2^; 4.0 m s^−2^; 550 m; 15.7 m s^−1^"},{"id":"week03.3.2.e2","title":"Reading a displacement–time graph","question":"The graph shows a boy walking away from his house and back. Find the velocity in stages P, Q and R, the total distance walked, and his final displacement.","img":{"src":"img/week03/st_walk","alt":"The graph shows a boy walking away from his house and back. Find the velocity in stages P, Q and R, the total distance walked, and his final displacement.","w":1094,"h":634},"steps":[{"label":"Stage P (gradient)","text":"⟪v=\\Delta s\\div \\Delta t=120\\div 40=\\boldsymbol{3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}}⟫ (away from the house)"},{"label":"Stage Q","text":"horizontal line → displacement constant → **at rest**, ⟪v=0⟫"},{"label":"Stage R (gradient)","text":"v = (0 − 120) ÷ (100 − 60) = −120 ÷ 40 = **−3.0 m s^−1^** (3.0 m s^−1^ back towards the house)"},{"label":"Total distance","text":"120 m out + 120 m back ⟪=\\boldsymbol{240\\,\\mathrm{m}}⟫"},{"label":"Final displacement","text":"he is back at the house, so displacement ⟪=\\boldsymbol{0\\,\\mathrm{m}}⟫"}],"answer":"P: 3.0 m s^−1^; Q: 0; R: ⟪-3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫; distance 240 m; displacement 0"},{"id":"week03.3.2.e3","title":"Ticker-tape speed and acceleration","question":"A ticker-timer makes 50 dots per second. On a tape from an accelerating trolley, an early 5-space strip is 2.0 cm long and a later 5-space strip is 6.0 cm long. The middles of the two strips are 0.50 s apart. Find the speed for each strip and the acceleration.","steps":[{"label":"Time for one strip","text":"⟪5\\times 0.02\\,\\mathrm{s}=0.10\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫"},{"label":"Early speed","text":"⟪u=0.020\\,\\mathrm{m}\\div 0.10\\,\\mathrm{s}=\\boldsymbol{0.20\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}}⟫   (convert ⟪2.0\\,\\mathrm{cm}=0.020\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ first)"},{"label":"Later speed","text":"⟪v=0.060\\,\\mathrm{m}\\div 0.10\\,\\mathrm{s}=\\boldsymbol{0.60\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}}⟫"},{"label":"Acceleration","text":"⟪a=(v-u)/t=(0.60-0.20)\\div 0.50=0.40\\div 0.50=\\boldsymbol{0.80\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}}⟫"}],"answer":"0.20 m s^−1^, 0.60 m s^−1^, ⟪a=0.80\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week03.3.2.p1","kind":"short","q":"What quantity is given by the gradient of a displacement–time graph?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"Velocity (1).","points":[{"text":"Velocity","marks":1}],"accept":["Velocity"],"section":"try"},{"id":"week03.3.2.p2","kind":"self","q":"What quantity is given by (a) the gradient and (b) the area under a velocity–time graph?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(a) Acceleration (1). (b) Displacement / distance travelled (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Acceleration","marks":1},{"text":"(b) Displacement / distance travelled","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Reading a velocity–time graph). Start like this: (a) Stage A: ⟪a=(v-u)/t=(20-0)\\div 10=\\boldsymbol{2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}}⟫","from":1}}],"practice":[{"id":"week03.3.2.p3","kind":"num","q":"Questions 3.2.3 to 3.2.7 refer to the velocity–time graph of a bus below. Calculate the acceleration of the bus in the first 6 s.","marks":2,"level":2,"img":{"src":"img/week03/vt_bus","alt":"Questions 3.2.3 to 3.2.7 refer to the velocity–time graph of a bus below. Calculate the acceleration of the bus in the first 6 s.","w":1052,"h":614},"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪a=(12-0)\\div 6⟫ (1) ⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪a=(12-0)\\div 6⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":2,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−2^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Reading a velocity–time graph). Start like this: (a) Stage A: ⟪a=(v-u)/t=(20-0)\\div 10=\\boldsymbol{2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week03.3.2.p4","kind":"self","q":"Calculate the deceleration of the bus between 16 s and 24 s.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪a=(0-12)\\div (24-16)=-12\\div 8⟫ (1) ⟪=-1.5\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫, i.e. deceleration 1.5 m s⁻² (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪a=(0-12)\\div (24-16)=-12\\div 8⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=-1.5\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫, i.e. deceleration 1.5 m s⁻²","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Reading a velocity–time graph). Start like this: (a) Stage A: ⟪a=(v-u)/t=(20-0)\\div 10=\\boldsymbol{2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week03.3.2.p5","kind":"num","q":"Calculate the total distance travelled by the bus.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Triangle 0–6 s: ⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 6\\times 12=36\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫; rectangle 6–16 s: ⟪10\\times 12=120\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫; triangle 16–24 s: ½ × 8 × 12 = 48 m (1 for any two areas correct, 1 for third).\nTotal ⟪=36+120+48=204\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1).\n(Alternative: trapezium ⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times (24+10)\\times 12=204\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫.)","points":[{"text":"Triangle 0–6 s: ⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 6\\times 12=36\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫; rectangle 6–16 s: ⟪10\\times 12=120\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫; triangle 16–24 s: ⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 8\\times 12=48\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"Total ⟪=36+120+48=204\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (Alternative: trapezium ⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times (24+10)\\times 12=204\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫.)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":204,"sf":3,"unit":"m","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}"},"conf":"low","section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Reading a velocity–time graph). Start like this: (a) Stage A: ⟪a=(v-u)/t=(20-0)\\div 10=\\boldsymbol{2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week03.3.2.p6","kind":"num","q":"Calculate the average speed of the bus for the whole 24 s.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"average speed = total distance ÷ total time ⟪=204\\div 24⟫ (1) ⟪=8.5\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"average speed = total distance ÷ total time ⟪=204\\div 24⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=8.5\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":8.5,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Reading a velocity–time graph). Start like this: (a) Stage A: ⟪a=(v-u)/t=(20-0)\\div 10=\\boldsymbol{2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week03.3.2.p7","kind":"self","q":"Describe the motion of the bus between 6 s and 16 s.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Constant (uniform) velocity (1) of 12 m s⁻¹, so acceleration is zero (1).","points":[{"text":"Constant (uniform) velocity","marks":1},{"text":"of 12 m s⁻¹, so acceleration is zero","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Reading a velocity–time graph). Start like this: (a) Stage A: ⟪a=(v-u)/t=(20-0)\\div 10=\\boldsymbol{2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}}⟫","from":1}},{"id":"week03.3.2.p8","kind":"self","q":"Sketch a displacement–time graph for a girl who walks away from home at a steady speed, stops for a while, then runs back home faster than she walked.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":7,"scheme":"Straight line sloping up from origin (1); horizontal section (1); straight line sloping DOWN to the time axis, steeper than the first section (1).","points":[{"text":"Straight line sloping up from origin","marks":1},{"text":"horizontal section","marks":1},{"text":"straight line sloping DOWN to the time axis, steeper than the first section","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Reading a displacement–time graph). Start like this: Stage P (gradient): ⟪v=\\Delta s\\div \\Delta t=120\\div 40=\\boldsymbol{3.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}}⟫ (away from the house)","from":2}}],"exit":[{"id":"week03.3.2.p9","kind":"num","q":"A 50 Hz ticker-timer is used. A 10-space strip of tape is 8.0 cm long. Calculate the average speed of the trolley during this strip.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"time ⟪=10\\times 0.02=0.20\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫ (1); speed ⟪=0.080\\,\\mathrm{m}\\div 0.20\\,\\mathrm{s}=0.40\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"time ⟪=10\\times 0.02=0.20\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"speed ⟪=0.080\\,\\mathrm{m}\\div 0.20\\,\\mathrm{s}=0.40\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":0.4,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"section":"exit"},{"id":"week03.3.2.p10","kind":"self","q":"A displacement–time graph for a falling stone is a curve. Explain how you would find the velocity of the stone at one particular time.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Draw a tangent to the curve at that time (1); calculate the gradient of the tangent ⟪(\\Delta s\\div \\Delta t)⟫ = velocity (1).","points":[{"text":"Draw a tangent to the curve at that time","marks":1},{"text":"calculate the gradient of the tangent ⟪(\\Delta s\\div \\Delta t)⟫ = velocity","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week03.3.2.w1","kind":"num","q":"A sprinter runs 100 m in 12.5 s. Calculate her average speed.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"average speed = distance ÷ time ⟪=100\\div 12.5⟫ (1) ⟪=8.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"average speed = distance ÷ time ⟪=100\\div 12.5⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=8.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":8,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week03.3.1.p4"},{"id":"week03.3.2.w2","kind":"self","q":"(a) Convert 90 km h^−1^ to m s^−1^.  (b) Convert 15 m s^−1^ to km h^−1^.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(a) ⟪90\\div 3.6=25\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).  (b) ⟪15\\times 3.6=54\\,\\mathrm{km}\\,\\mathrm{h}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) ⟪90\\div 3.6=25\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"(b) ⟪15\\times 3.6=54\\,\\mathrm{km}\\,\\mathrm{h}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week03.3.1.p3"},{"id":"week03.3.2.w3","kind":"self","q":"Distinguish between **distance** and **displacement**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Distance is the total length of path travelled; it is a scalar (1). Displacement is the straight-line distance from start to finish in a stated direction; it is a vector (1).","points":[{"text":"Distance is the total length of path travelled; it is a scalar","marks":1},{"text":"Displacement is the straight-line distance from start to finish in a stated direction; it is a vector","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week03.3.1.p1"}],"n":16},{"id":"week03.3.3","kind":"unit","unit":"3.3","title":"From Aristotle to Newton: The Three Laws of Motion","titleText":"From Aristotle to Newton: The Three Laws of Motion","goals":[{"code":"A4.3","text":"Discuss Aristotle's arguments about motion and why they were replaced"},{"code":"A4.4","text":"State Newton's three laws of motion"}],"codes":["A4.3","A4.4"],"words":[{"term":"Speed","def":"Rate of change of distance (scalar)."},{"term":"Velocity","def":"Rate of change of displacement; speed in a stated direction (vector)."},{"term":"Acceleration","def":"Rate of change of velocity (vector), m s^−2^."},{"term":"Uniform","def":"Constant, not changing (e.g. uniform acceleration)."},{"term":"Resultant force","def":"The single force with the same effect as all the forces acting together."},{"term":"Inertia","def":"Reluctance of a body to change its state of rest or motion; depends on mass."},{"term":"Friction","def":"Force opposing motion between surfaces in contact."},{"term":"Momentum","def":"Mass × velocity (vector), kg m s^−1^."},{"term":"Recoil","def":"Backward motion of a gun (or other body) when it pushes something forwards."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"h","text":"Aristotle's ideas about motion (Greece, 384–322 BC)"},{"type":"p","text":"For almost 2000 years people believed the ideas of the Greek thinker **Aristotle**. He built his ideas from **everyday observation and reasoning**, not from careful experiments. His main arguments were:"},{"type":"bullets","items":["The **natural state** of an object on Earth is **rest**. Moving objects naturally slow down and stop.","A **force is needed to KEEP an object moving**. Remove the force (stop pushing the cart) and the object stops. A constant force produces a constant speed.","**Heavier objects fall faster** than lighter ones — the speed of fall is proportional to the weight.","There were two kinds of motion: **natural motion** (things moving to their \"natural place\", e.g. a stone falling down, smoke rising) and **violent (forced) motion** caused by a push or pull.","A vacuum (empty space) could not exist."]},{"type":"h","text":"Why Aristotle's ideas were replaced"},{"type":"bullets","items":["**Galileo** (Week 1) TESTED the ideas. He rolled balls down inclined planes and showed that falling objects speed up uniformly and, apart from air resistance, **all objects fall with the same acceleration** whatever their mass.","Galileo noticed that a ball rolling down one slope rolls up an opposite slope to almost the same height. If the second slope were flat and there were no **friction**, the ball would roll **forever**. So objects slow down because of friction — **no force is needed to keep something moving**.","**Isaac Newton** (1642–1727) built on Galileo's work and wrote his **three laws of motion** (1687), which correctly predict motion and replaced Aristotle's ideas.","Modern proof: on the Moon (no air), an astronaut dropped a hammer and a feather together and they hit the ground at the same time."]},{"type":"table","headers":["Question","Aristotle said…","Newton / Galileo showed…"],"rows":[["What keeps an object moving?","A force must keep pushing it","Nothing — it keeps moving by itself (inertia) unless a resultant force acts"],["Why do moving things stop?","Rest is their natural state","A force (friction / air resistance) stops them"],["Do heavy objects fall faster?","Yes, in proportion to weight","No — without air resistance all fall with the same acceleration g"],["How was it decided?","Reasoning, everyday observation","Experiments, measurement and mathematics"]]},{"type":"tip","text":"For a 3-mark \"Aristotle\" question write: (1) his belief (a force is needed to keep a body moving / heavy bodies fall faster), (2) it was based on reasoning not experiment, (3) Galileo/Newton showed by experiment that objects keep moving unless friction acts / all bodies fall at the same rate."},{"type":"h","text":"Newton's First Law — the law of inertia"},{"type":"def","term":"Newton's first law","text":"Every body continues in its state of **rest** or of **uniform motion in a straight line** unless it is acted on by a **resultant (unbalanced) external force**."},{"type":"def","term":"Inertia","text":"the reluctance (tendency to resist) of a body to change its state of rest or uniform motion. The **greater the mass, the greater the inertia**."},{"type":"p","text":"So if the forces on a body are **balanced** (resultant ⟪=0⟫), it either stays still or keeps moving at constant velocity. Examples:"},{"type":"bullets","items":["When a bus brakes suddenly, the passengers **lurch forward**: their bodies keep moving forward because of inertia. **Seat belts** provide the force to stop them.","When a bus starts suddenly, standing passengers fall **backwards**: their bodies tend to stay at rest while the bus moves forward.","A coin on a card on top of a glass drops into the glass when the card is flicked away quickly.","A spacecraft far out in space keeps moving at constant velocity with its engines OFF.","Headrests in cars protect the neck in a collision from behind: the body is pushed forward, but the head's inertia keeps it behind."]},{"type":"h","text":"Newton's Second Law — force causes acceleration"},{"type":"def","term":"Newton's second law","text":"The **rate of change of momentum** of a body is **proportional to the resultant force** acting on it and takes place in the **direction of the force**. For a fixed mass this gives ⟪\\boldsymbol{F=ma}⟫."},{"type":"formula","tex":"F=ma","where":["⟪F⟫ = resultant (unbalanced) force (N); m = mass (kg); a = acceleration (m s^−2^)","The acceleration is in the SAME direction as the resultant force.","**The newton**: 1 N is the force that gives a mass of 1 kg an acceleration of 1 m s^−2^. So ⟪1\\,\\mathrm{N}=1\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫."],"label":"NEWTON'S SECOND LAW"},{"type":"h","text":"Newton's Third Law — forces come in pairs"},{"type":"def","term":"Newton's third law","text":"If body A exerts a force on body B, then body B exerts a force on body A that is **equal in size and opposite in direction**. (\"For every action there is an equal and opposite reaction.\")"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week03/third_law","alt":"Figure 3.5 — Newton's third law pairs: a rocket and its exhaust gases; a book and a table","caption":"Figure 3.5 — Newton's third law pairs: a rocket and its exhaust gases; a book and a table","width":5,"w":1220,"h":553},{"type":"p","text":"The two forces in a third-law pair are always:"},{"type":"bullets","items":["**equal** in size;","**opposite** in direction;","acting on **two DIFFERENT bodies** (so they never cancel each other);","the **same type** of force (both contact, or both gravitational, etc.);","acting at the **same time**."]},{"type":"p","text":"Examples: when you **walk**, your foot pushes the ground backwards and the ground pushes you forwards. A **swimmer** pushes water backwards; the water pushes the swimmer forwards. A **rocket** pushes hot gases out downwards; the gases push the rocket upwards. A **gun** pushes the bullet forwards; the bullet pushes the gun backwards (recoil)."},{"type":"warn","text":"The weight of a book (Earth pulls book down) and the push of the table on the book (table pushes book up) are equal and opposite, but they are **NOT** a third-law pair — they both act on the SAME body (the book) and are different types of force. The third-law partner of the book's weight is the **book pulling the Earth upwards**."}],"examples":[{"id":"week03.3.3.e1","title":"Explaining with Newton's first law","question":"A passenger is not wearing a seat belt. The car stops suddenly in a crash. Use Newton's first law to explain what happens to the passenger.","steps":[{"label":"State the law (or the idea)","text":"A body keeps moving with uniform velocity unless a resultant force acts on it (inertia)."},{"label":"Apply it","text":"The crash puts a large force on the CAR, so the car stops. No force acts on the passenger to stop him."},{"label":"Conclusion","text":"So the passenger **keeps moving forward** at the car's original speed and hits the dashboard / windscreen. A seat belt supplies the force needed to stop him with the car."}],"answer":"The passenger continues moving forward (inertia) because no force stops him, and hits the front of the car."},{"id":"week03.3.3.e2","title":"Identifying a third-law pair","question":"An apple of weight 1.5 N hangs from a tree. (a) What exerts the weight force, and on what? (b) State the size and direction of the third-law partner of this force and the body it acts on.","steps":[{"label":"(a)","text":"The weight is the **Earth** pulling the **apple** downwards (gravitational force)."},{"label":"(b) Swap the bodies","text":"So the partner is the **apple pulling the Earth**: **1.5 N, upwards**, a gravitational force acting **on the Earth**."}],"answer":"1.5 N upwards, gravitational pull of the apple on the Earth"},{"id":"week03.3.3.e3","title":"Aristotle vs Galileo","question":"A heavy stone and a light pebble are dropped together from the same height (air resistance is negligible). What would Aristotle have predicted, and what actually happens? Explain.","steps":[{"label":"Aristotle","text":"Heavier objects fall faster, so the stone would land first (in about half the time if it were twice as heavy)."},{"label":"What happens","text":"Both land at the **same time**."},{"label":"Why","text":"All objects fall with the same acceleration ⟪g\\approx 10\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫ when air resistance is negligible. Galileo showed this by experiment; Aristotle never tested his idea."}],"answer":"Aristotle: stone first. Reality: both land together (same acceleration g)."}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week03.3.3.p1","kind":"self","q":"State TWO of Aristotle's ideas about motion.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Any two: a force is needed to keep a body moving (1); the natural state of objects is rest (1); heavier objects fall faster than lighter ones (1); natural vs violent motion (1).","points":[{"text":"Any two: a force is needed to keep a body moving","marks":1},{"text":"the natural state of objects is rest","marks":1},{"text":"heavier objects fall faster than lighter ones","marks":1},{"text":"natural vs violent motion","marks":1}],"conf":"low","section":"try"},{"id":"week03.3.3.p2","kind":"self","q":"Explain why Aristotle's ideas about motion were eventually replaced.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Aristotle's ideas were based on reasoning/observation, not experiment (1). Galileo tested them by experiment (inclined planes) and showed objects fall at the same rate / keep moving if there is no friction (1). Newton's laws explained and predicted motion accurately, so they replaced Aristotle's ideas (1).","points":[{"text":"Aristotle's ideas were based on reasoning/observation, not experiment","marks":1},{"text":"Galileo tested them by experiment (inclined planes) and showed objects fall at the same rate / keep moving if there is no friction","marks":1},{"text":"Newton's laws explained and predicted motion accurately, so they replaced Aristotle's ideas","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Aristotle vs Galileo). Start like this: Aristotle: Heavier objects fall faster, so the stone would land first (in about half the time if it were twice as heavy).","from":3}}],"practice":[{"id":"week03.3.3.p3","kind":"self","q":"State Newton's first law of motion.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"A body continues in its state of rest or uniform motion in a straight line (1) unless acted on by a resultant/unbalanced (external) force (1).","points":[{"text":"A body continues in its state of rest or uniform motion in a straight line","marks":1},{"text":"unless acted on by a resultant/unbalanced (external) force","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Explaining with Newton's first law). Start like this: State the law (or the idea): A body keeps moving with uniform velocity unless a resultant force acts on it (inertia).","from":1}},{"id":"week03.3.3.p4","kind":"self","q":"Define **inertia** and state what it depends on.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"The tendency of a body to resist a change in its state of rest or motion (1). It depends on mass — greater mass, greater inertia (1).","points":[{"text":"The tendency of a body to resist a change in its state of rest or motion","marks":1},{"text":"It depends on mass — greater mass, greater inertia","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Identifying a third-law pair). Start like this: (a): The weight is the **Earth** pulling the **apple** downwards (gravitational force).","from":2}},{"id":"week03.3.3.p5","kind":"self","q":"Standing passengers on a bus fall backwards when the bus moves off suddenly. Explain why.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Their bodies tend to remain at rest because of inertia (Newton's 1st law) (1); the bus (and their feet) move forward beneath them, so they fall backwards relative to the bus (1).","points":[{"text":"Their bodies tend to remain at rest because of inertia (Newton's 1st law)","marks":1},{"text":"the bus (and their feet) move forward beneath them, so they fall backwards relative to the bus","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Explaining with Newton's first law). Start like this: State the law (or the idea): A body keeps moving with uniform velocity unless a resultant force acts on it (inertia).","from":1}},{"id":"week03.3.3.p6","kind":"self","q":"State Newton's third law of motion.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"If body A exerts a force on body B, body B exerts a force on A (1) that is equal in magnitude and opposite in direction (1).","points":[{"text":"If body A exerts a force on body B, body B exerts a force on A","marks":1},{"text":"that is equal in magnitude and opposite in direction","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Identifying a third-law pair). Start like this: (a): The weight is the **Earth** pulling the **apple** downwards (gravitational force).","from":2}},{"id":"week03.3.3.p7","kind":"self","q":"Use Newton's third law to explain how a swimmer moves forward.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"The swimmer pushes the water backwards (1); the water pushes the swimmer forwards with an equal and opposite force (1).","points":[{"text":"The swimmer pushes the water backwards","marks":1},{"text":"the water pushes the swimmer forwards with an equal and opposite force","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Explaining with Newton's first law). Start like this: State the law (or the idea): A body keeps moving with uniform velocity unless a resultant force acts on it (inertia).","from":1}}],"exit":[{"id":"week03.3.3.p8","kind":"self","q":"A book rests on a table. Kemar says: \"The weight of the book and the upward push of the table are a Newton's third law pair.\" Explain why he is wrong.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Both forces act on the SAME body (the book) — a third-law pair acts on two different bodies (1). They are different types of force (gravitational and contact) (1). The true partner of the weight is the gravitational pull of the book on the Earth, upwards (1).","points":[{"text":"Both forces act on the SAME body (the book) — a third-law pair acts on two different bodies","marks":1},{"text":"They are different types of force (gravitational and contact)","marks":1},{"text":"The true partner of the weight is the gravitational pull of the book on the Earth, upwards","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week03.3.3.p9","kind":"self","q":"Define the **newton**.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"The force which gives a mass of 1 kg (1) an acceleration of 1 m s⁻² (1).","points":[{"text":"The force which gives a mass of 1 kg","marks":1},{"text":"an acceleration of 1 m s⁻²","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week03.3.3.w1","kind":"num","q":"Calculate the average speed of the bus for the whole 24 s.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"average speed = total distance ÷ total time ⟪=204\\div 24⟫ (1) ⟪=8.5\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"average speed = total distance ÷ total time ⟪=204\\div 24⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=8.5\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":8.5,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"section":"warmup","src":"week03.3.2.p6"},{"id":"week03.3.3.w2","kind":"self","q":"What quantity is given by (a) the gradient and (b) the area under a velocity–time graph?","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"(a) Acceleration (1). (b) Displacement / distance travelled (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Acceleration","marks":1},{"text":"(b) Displacement / distance travelled","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week03.3.2.p2"},{"id":"week03.3.3.w3","kind":"short","q":"What quantity is given by the gradient of a displacement–time graph?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"Velocity (1).","points":[{"text":"Velocity","marks":1}],"accept":["Velocity"],"section":"warmup","src":"week03.3.2.p1"}],"n":17},{"id":"week03.3.4","kind":"unit","unit":"3.4","title":"Using ⟪F=ma⟫: Resultant Force, Friction and Terminal Velocity","titleText":"Using F = ma: Resultant Force, Friction and Terminal Velocity","goals":[{"code":"A4.5","text":"Use Newton's laws (including ⟪F=ma⟫) to explain and calculate motion in dynamic systems"}],"codes":["A4.5"],"words":[{"term":"Speed","def":"Rate of change of distance (scalar)."},{"term":"Velocity","def":"Rate of change of displacement; speed in a stated direction (vector)."},{"term":"Acceleration","def":"Rate of change of velocity (vector), m s^−2^."},{"term":"Resultant force","def":"The single force with the same effect as all the forces acting together."},{"term":"Friction","def":"Force opposing motion between surfaces in contact."},{"term":"Terminal velocity","def":"Constant velocity of a falling body when drag equals weight."},{"term":"Free-body diagram","def":"Sketch of one body showing every force acting on it as an arrow."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"p","text":"To use Newton's second law you ALWAYS need the **resultant force** — the single force that has the same effect as all the forces together."},{"type":"h","text":"Method for every ⟪F=ma⟫ problem"},{"type":"steps","items":["Draw a **free-body diagram**: the object as a box with an arrow for EVERY force on it (weight, driving force, friction, air resistance, normal reaction, tension).","Choose a positive direction (usually the direction of motion).","Find the **resultant force**: forces in the + direction minus forces in the opposite direction.","Use ⟪\\boldsymbol{F=ma}⟫ with that resultant force. Rearrange if needed: ⟪a=F/m⟫ or ⟪m=F/a⟫.","Give units: N, kg, m s^−2^."]},{"type":"img","src":"img/week03/car_forces","alt":"Figure 3.6 — Free-body diagram (horizontal forces) for an accelerating car","caption":"Figure 3.6 — Free-body diagram (horizontal forces) for an accelerating car","width":4.2,"w":1014,"h":369},{"type":"table","headers":["Forces are…","Resultant force","What happens (Newton's laws)"],"rows":[["Balanced","zero","At rest stays at rest; moving keeps a CONSTANT velocity (1st law)"],["Unbalanced","not zero","Accelerates in the direction of the resultant: ⟪a=F/m\\ (2ndlaw)⟫"]]},{"type":"remember","text":"Moving at a constant speed in a straight line means the forces are BALANCED — e.g. driving force = friction. You do NOT need an unbalanced force to keep moving (that was Aristotle's mistake)."},{"type":"h","text":"Friction and air resistance"},{"type":"bullets","items":["**Friction** acts where two surfaces rub. It always acts **opposite to the motion** (or attempted motion).","**Air resistance (drag)** is friction from the air. It **increases as speed increases**.","Friction is useful (walking, brakes, tyres gripping the road) and a nuisance (wear, heating, wasted energy). Reduce it with lubricants, ball-bearings, streamlining."]},{"type":"h","text":"Falling objects and terminal velocity (a dynamic system)"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week03/skydiver_fbd","alt":"Figure 3.7 — Forces on a falling skydiver","caption":"Figure 3.7 — Forces on a falling skydiver","width":1.6,"w":452,"h":614},{"type":"img","src":"img/week03/skydiver_vt","alt":"Figure 3.8 — Velocity–time graph for a skydiver","caption":"Figure 3.8 — Velocity–time graph for a skydiver","width":4.4,"w":1134,"h":634},{"type":"table","headers":["Stage","Forces","Motion"],"rows":[["A — just after jumping","weight ≫ air resistance (air resistance small at low speed)","large resultant downward → large acceleration (≈ 10 m s^−2^ at first)"],["A → B — speeding up","air resistance increases with speed; resultant decreases","acceleration decreases (graph gets less steep)"],["B — terminal velocity","air resistance = weight; resultant ⟪=0⟫","constant (terminal) velocity, ⟪a=0⟫"],["C — parachute opens","air resistance ≫ weight; resultant UPWARD","decelerates; air resistance falls as speed falls"],["D — new terminal velocity","air resistance = weight again","lower constant velocity — safe landing"]]},{"type":"def","term":"Terminal velocity","text":"the constant (maximum) velocity reached by a falling body when the air resistance (drag) upward equals its weight, so the resultant force is zero."},{"type":"h","text":"Investigating ⟪F=ma⟫ with a trolley"},{"type":"steps","items":["**Friction-compensate** the runway: raise one end until a trolley, given a small push, rolls down at a **constant speed** (equal dot spacing on ticker tape). Now friction is balanced by part of the weight.","Pull the trolley with **one elastic cord stretched by a fixed length** (constant force, call it 1 unit). Use a ticker-timer (or light gates) to find the acceleration.","Repeat with 2, 3, 4 identical cords side by side, each stretched by the same length (2, 3, 4 units of force). Keep the mass constant.","**Results**: plot a against F → a **straight line through the origin**, so ⟪\\boldsymbol{a\\propto F}⟫ (mass constant).","Then keep the force constant and stack 1, 2, 3 trolleys: a against 1/m is a straight line through the origin, so ⟪\\boldsymbol{a\\propto 1/m}⟫. Together these give ⟪F=ma⟫.","**Precautions**: keep cords stretched by the same length throughout; repeat runs and average; catch the trolley at the end of the runway."]},{"type":"tip","text":"In a calculation the force in ⟪F=ma⟫ must be the RESULTANT force. If a question gives you a driving force AND friction, subtract first. Examiners give the method mark for \"resultant ⟪=3000-600⟫\"."}],"examples":[{"id":"week03.3.4.e1","title":"Resultant force then acceleration","question":"A car of mass 1200 kg has a driving force of 3000 N. Friction and air resistance together are 600 N. Calculate the acceleration of the car.","img":{"src":"img/week03/car_forces","alt":"A car of mass 1200 kg has a driving force of 3000 N. Friction and air resistance together are 600 N. Calculate the acceleration of the car.","w":1014,"h":369},"steps":[{"label":"Resultant force","text":"⟪F=3000-600=2400\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (forwards)"},{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪F=ma⟫, so ⟪a=F/m⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪a=2400/1200⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪a=\\boldsymbol{2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}}⟫ (forwards)"}],"answer":"2.0 m s^−2^"},{"id":"week03.3.4.e2","title":"Braking force (two-step problem)","question":"A car of mass 900 kg slows from 20 m s^−1^ to rest in 4.0 s. Calculate the braking force.","steps":[{"label":"Step 1: acceleration","text":"⟪a=(v-u)/t=(0-20)/4.0=-5.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫"},{"label":"Step 2: force","text":"⟪F=ma=900\\times (-5.0)=-4500\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"Braking force ⟪=\\boldsymbol{4500\\,\\mathrm{N}}⟫ (the minus sign shows it acts backwards, against the motion)"}],"answer":"4500 N (acting backwards)"},{"id":"week03.3.4.e3","title":"Falling skydiver","question":"A skydiver of mass 80 kg falls. At one moment the air resistance is 300 N. (a) Calculate her acceleration. (b) What is the air resistance when she reaches terminal velocity?","img":{"src":"img/week03/skydiver_fbd","alt":"A skydiver of mass 80 kg falls. At one moment the air resistance is 300 N. (a) Calculate her acceleration. (b) What is the air resistance when she reaches terminal velocity?","w":452,"h":614},"steps":[{"label":"Weight","text":"⟪W=mg=80\\times 10=800\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (down)"},{"label":"Resultant","text":"⟪F=800-300=500\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (down)"},{"label":"(a) Acceleration","text":"⟪a=F/m=500/80=6.25\\approx \\boldsymbol{6.3\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}}⟫ downwards"},{"label":"(b) Terminal velocity","text":"resultant ⟪=0⟫, so air resistance = weight ⟪=\\boldsymbol{800\\,\\mathrm{N}}⟫"}],"answer":"6.3 m s^−2^ downwards; 800 N"},{"id":"week03.3.4.e4","title":"Two connected bodies (a dynamic system)","question":"A force of 20 N pushes a 3.0 kg block, which pushes a 2.0 kg block in front of it along a smooth floor. Find (a) the acceleration of the blocks, (b) the force the 3.0 kg block exerts on the 2.0 kg block.","img":{"src":"img/week03/two_blocks","alt":"A force of 20 N pushes a 3.0 kg block, which pushes a 2.0 kg block in front of it along a smooth floor. Find (a) the acceleration of the blocks, (b) the force the 3.0 kg block exerts on the 2.0 kg block.","w":878,"h":314},"steps":[{"label":"(a) Treat as one body","text":"total mass ⟪=3.0+2.0=5.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}⟫; resultant force ⟪=20\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (no friction)"},{"label":"Acceleration","text":"⟪a=F/m=20/5.0=\\boldsymbol{4.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}}⟫"},{"label":"(b) Look at the 2.0 kg block alone","text":"the only horizontal force on it is the push from the 3.0 kg block, and it accelerates at 4.0 m s^−2^"},{"label":"Force","text":"⟪F=ma=2.0\\times 4.0=\\boldsymbol{8.0\\,\\mathrm{N}}⟫"},{"label":"Check (3rd law)","text":"the 2.0 kg block pushes back on the 3.0 kg block with 8.0 N, so the resultant on the 3.0 kg block ⟪=20-8.0=12\\,\\mathrm{N}=3.0\\times 4.0⟫ ✓"}],"answer":"4.0 m s^−2^; 8.0 N"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week03.3.4.p1","kind":"self","q":"Write down the equation for Newton's second law and give the unit of each quantity.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪F=ma⟫ (1); F in newtons (N), m in kilograms (kg), a in m s⁻² (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪F=ma⟫","marks":1},{"text":"F in newtons (N), m in kilograms (kg), a in m s⁻²","marks":1}],"section":"try"},{"id":"week03.3.4.p2","kind":"self","q":"Calculate the resultant force needed to give a 2.5 kg mass an acceleration of 4.0 m s^−2^.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪F=ma=2.5\\times 4.0⟫ (1) ⟪=10\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪F=ma=2.5\\times 4.0⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=10\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Resultant force then acceleration). Start like this: Resultant force: ⟪F=3000-600=2400\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (forwards)","from":1}}],"practice":[{"id":"week03.3.4.p3","kind":"num","q":"A resultant force of 15 N gives a trolley an acceleration of 3.0 m s^−2^. Calculate the mass of the trolley.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪m=F/a=15\\div 3.0⟫ (1) ⟪=5.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪m=F/a=15\\div 3.0⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=5.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":5,"sf":2,"unit":"kg","unitTex":"\\mathrm{kg}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Resultant force then acceleration). Start like this: Resultant force: ⟪F=3000-600=2400\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (forwards)","from":1}},{"id":"week03.3.4.p4","kind":"num","q":"A boat of mass 400 kg has an engine force of 1500 N. The water resistance is 700 N. Calculate the acceleration of the boat.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Resultant ⟪=1500-700=800\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (1); ⟪a=F/m=800\\div 400⟫ (1) ⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Resultant ⟪=1500-700=800\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪a=F/m=800\\div 400⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":2,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−2^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}"},"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Resultant force then acceleration). Start like this: Resultant force: ⟪F=3000-600=2400\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (forwards)","from":1}},{"id":"week03.3.4.p5","kind":"self","q":"A car of mass 1000 kg travels at a constant velocity of 25 m s^−1^ with a driving force of 2000 N. State the total resistive force and explain your answer.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"2000 N (1). Constant velocity → acceleration zero → resultant force zero, so forces are balanced (Newton's 1st law) (1).","points":[{"text":"2000 N","marks":1},{"text":"Constant velocity → acceleration zero → resultant force zero, so forces are balanced (Newton's 1st law)","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Resultant force then acceleration). Start like this: Resultant force: ⟪F=3000-600=2400\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (forwards)","from":1}},{"id":"week03.3.4.p6","kind":"num","q":"A lorry of mass 5000 kg slows down from 15 m s^−1^ to rest in 10 s. Calculate the braking force.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪a=(0-15)/10=-1.5\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫ (1); ⟪F=ma=5000\\times 1.5⟫ (1) ⟪=7500\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (acting backwards) (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪a=(0-15)/10=-1.5\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪F=ma=5000\\times 1.5⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=7500\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (acting backwards)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":7500,"sf":2,"unit":"N","unitTex":"\\mathrm{N}"},"conf":"low","section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Braking force (two-step problem)). Start like this: Step 1: acceleration: ⟪a=(v-u)/t=(0-20)/4.0=-5.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫","from":2}},{"id":"week03.3.4.p7","kind":"self","q":"A skydiver jumps from a plane. Explain, in terms of the forces acting, why she first accelerates and then reaches a terminal velocity.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":6,"scheme":"At first the weight is much greater than air resistance, so there is a large downward resultant force and she accelerates (1). As her speed increases, the air resistance increases (1), so the resultant force and the acceleration decrease (1). Eventually air resistance equals weight, the resultant is zero and she falls at constant (terminal) velocity (1).","points":[{"text":"At first the weight is much greater than air resistance, so there is a large downward resultant force and she accelerates","marks":1},{"text":"As her speed increases, the air resistance increases","marks":1},{"text":"so the resultant force and the acceleration decrease","marks":1},{"text":"Eventually air resistance equals weight, the resultant is zero and she falls at constant (terminal) velocity","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Falling skydiver). Start like this: Weight: ⟪W=mg=80\\times 10=800\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (down)","from":3}},{"id":"week03.3.4.p8","kind":"num","q":"A rocket of mass 2000 kg is launched vertically. The engines produce an upward thrust of 30 000 N. Calculate the initial acceleration of the rocket.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪W=mg=2000\\times 10=20\\,000\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (1); resultant ⟪=30\\,000-20\\,000=10\\,000\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ upward (1); ⟪a=10\\,000\\div 2000=5.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪W=mg=2000\\times 10=20\\,000\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"resultant ⟪=30\\,000-20\\,000=10\\,000\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ upward","marks":1},{"text":"⟪a=10\\,000\\div 2000=5.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":5,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−2^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}"},"conf":"low","section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Resultant force then acceleration). Start like this: Resultant force: ⟪F=3000-600=2400\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (forwards)","from":1}}],"exit":[{"id":"week03.3.4.p9","kind":"num","q":"A 60 kg man stands in a lift that is accelerating UPWARDS at 2.0 m s^−2^. Calculate the upward force the floor exerts on him. (Hint: resultant = floor force − weight.)","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪W=60\\times 10=600\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (1); resultant needed ⟪=ma=60\\times 2.0=120\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ upward (1); floor force ⟪R=600+120=720\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪W=60\\times 10=600\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"resultant needed ⟪=ma=60\\times 2.0=120\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ upward","marks":1},{"text":"floor force ⟪R=600+120=720\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":720,"sf":2,"unit":"N","unitTex":"\\mathrm{N}"},"conf":"low","section":"exit"},{"id":"week03.3.4.p10","kind":"self","q":"In the trolley experiment to investigate ⟪F=ma⟫, (a) why is the runway friction-compensated, and (b) what graph shows that ⟪a\\propto F⟫?","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"(a) So that friction is balanced (1) and the elastic cords provide the resultant force (1). (b) A graph of a against F is a straight line through the origin (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) So that friction is balanced","marks":1},{"text":"and the elastic cords provide the resultant force","marks":1},{"text":"(b) A graph of a against F is a straight line through the origin","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week03.3.4.w1","kind":"self","q":"Define **inertia** and state what it depends on.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"The tendency of a body to resist a change in its state of rest or motion (1). It depends on mass — greater mass, greater inertia (1).","points":[{"text":"The tendency of a body to resist a change in its state of rest or motion","marks":1},{"text":"It depends on mass — greater mass, greater inertia","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week03.3.3.p4"},{"id":"week03.3.4.w2","kind":"self","q":"State TWO of Aristotle's ideas about motion.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Any two: a force is needed to keep a body moving (1); the natural state of objects is rest (1); heavier objects fall faster than lighter ones (1); natural vs violent motion (1).","points":[{"text":"Any two: a force is needed to keep a body moving","marks":1},{"text":"the natural state of objects is rest","marks":1},{"text":"heavier objects fall faster than lighter ones","marks":1},{"text":"natural vs violent motion","marks":1}],"conf":"low","section":"warmup","src":"week03.3.3.p1"},{"id":"week03.3.4.w3","kind":"self","q":"Standing passengers on a bus fall backwards when the bus moves off suddenly. Explain why.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Their bodies tend to remain at rest because of inertia (Newton's 1st law) (1); the bus (and their feet) move forward beneath them, so they fall backwards relative to the bus (1).","points":[{"text":"Their bodies tend to remain at rest because of inertia (Newton's 1st law)","marks":1},{"text":"the bus (and their feet) move forward beneath them, so they fall backwards relative to the bus","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week03.3.3.p5"}],"n":18},{"id":"week03.3.5","kind":"unit","unit":"3.5","title":"Momentum and Its Conservation","titleText":"Momentum and Its Conservation","goals":[{"code":"A4.6","text":"Define linear momentum and use ⟪p=mv⟫"},{"code":"A4.7","text":"Identify situations in which linear momentum is conserved (collisions, explosions, recoil, rockets)"},{"code":"A4.8","text":"Apply the law of conservation of linear momentum to solve problems"}],"codes":["A4.6","A4.7","A4.8"],"words":[{"term":"Speed","def":"Rate of change of distance (scalar)."},{"term":"Velocity","def":"Rate of change of displacement; speed in a stated direction (vector)."},{"term":"Resultant force","def":"The single force with the same effect as all the forces acting together."},{"term":"Friction","def":"Force opposing motion between surfaces in contact."},{"term":"Momentum","def":"Mass × velocity (vector), kg m s^−1^."},{"term":"Impulse","def":"Force × time = change in momentum, N s."},{"term":"Recoil","def":"Backward motion of a gun (or other body) when it pushes something forwards."},{"term":"Friction-compensated runway","def":"Runway tilted just enough that a trolley rolls at constant speed, so friction is balanced."}],"learn":{"notes":[{"type":"def","term":"Linear momentum","text":"the product of a body's **mass and its velocity**. It is a **vector**, with the same direction as the velocity."},{"type":"formula","tex":"p=mv","where":["⟪p⟫ = momentum (kg m s^−1^, which is the same as N s); m = mass (kg); v = velocity (m s^−1^)","Example: a 0.15 kg cricket ball moving at 40 m s^−1^ has ⟪p=0.15\\times 40=6.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫.","Because it is a vector, a body moving LEFT has NEGATIVE momentum if right is taken as positive."],"label":"MOMENTUM"},{"type":"h","text":"Newton's second law in momentum form"},{"type":"formula","tex":"F=(mv-mu)/t=\\text{ change in momentum }\\div \\text{ time}","where":["⟪F⟫ = resultant force (N); ⟪mv-mu⟫ = change in momentum Δp (kg m s^−1^); t = time the force acts (s)","Rearranged: ⟪\\boldsymbol{F\\times t=\\Delta p}⟫. The product ⟪F\\times t⟫ is called the **impulse** (unit N s).","For the SAME change in momentum, a LONGER time means a SMALLER force. This is the physics of safety features."],"label":"FORCE AND MOMENTUM"},{"type":"table","headers":["Safety feature / action","How it works"],"rows":[["Crumple zones in cars","front and back crush slowly, increasing the stopping time → smaller force on passengers"],["Air bags and seat belts","stretch / inflate so the passenger stops over a longer time → smaller force; spread force over a bigger area"],["Helmets, padded dashboards, gym mats, sand pits","soft material squashes → longer time of impact → smaller force"],["Bending your knees when you land","increases the time to stop → smaller force on your legs"]]},{"type":"h","text":"The principle of conservation of linear momentum"},{"type":"def","term":"Principle of conservation of linear momentum","text":"In a **closed (isolated) system**, where **no external resultant force** acts, the **total momentum before** a collision or explosion **equals the total momentum after** it."},{"type":"formula","tex":"m_{1}u_{1}+m_{2}u_{2}=m_{1}v_{1}+m_{2}v_{2}","where":["⟪u⟫ = velocities BEFORE; v = velocities AFTER. Use + for one direction and − for the opposite direction.","If the bodies **stick together** after: ⟪m_{1}u_{1}+m_{2}u_{2}=(m_{1}+m_{2})v⟫","If they start **at rest** (explosion/recoil): ⟪0=m_{1}v_{1}+m_{2}v_{2}⟫, so they move off in OPPOSITE directions."],"label":"CONSERVATION OF MOMENTUM"},{"type":"p","text":"**Why is momentum conserved?** In a collision, by Newton's third law, A pushes B and B pushes A with equal and opposite forces for the same time. So A loses exactly as much momentum as B gains."},{"type":"h","text":"Situations where momentum is conserved"},{"type":"table","headers":["Situation","What happens","Example"],"rows":[["Collision — bodies stick together","they move off together with one common velocity","railway trucks coupling; a car hitting another and locking bumpers"],["Collision — bodies bounce apart","each has its own velocity after; total momentum unchanged","snooker balls; a ball hitting another ball"],["Explosion / recoil","total momentum 0 before → 0 after; parts fly apart in opposite directions","rifle recoils when fired; cannon; two skaters pushing apart; a person jumping off a small boat"],["Rocket and jet propulsion","gas is pushed out backwards with momentum; rocket gains equal momentum forwards","rockets in space; jet engines; a balloon released untied; a squid"]]},{"type":"remember","text":"Momentum is conserved in ALL collisions and explosions (when no external force acts). Kinetic energy is often NOT conserved — some becomes heat and sound (Week 4)."},{"type":"h","text":"Experiment: verifying the conservation of momentum"},{"type":"img","src":"img/week03/momentum_apparatus","alt":"Figure 3.9 — Trolley collision on a friction-compensated runway","caption":"Figure 3.9 — Trolley collision on a friction-compensated runway","width":5.2,"w":1021,"h":548},{"type":"steps","items":["Find the masses of trolleys A and B with a balance.","Friction-compensate the runway (a gentle push gives constant speed — evenly spaced dots).","Attach a ticker tape to trolley A. Put B at rest. A has a pin at the front, B has a cork, so they stick together on impact.","Give A a push. It hits B and they move off together.","From the tape, find A's velocity BEFORE (5-space strip before the collision, ÷ 0.10 s) and the common velocity AFTER (5-space strip after).","Calculate total momentum before ⟪=m_{A}u_{A}⟫ and after ⟪=(m_{A}+m_{B})v⟫. **Result**: they are equal (within experimental error).","Repeat with different masses (stack trolleys) and different starting speeds."]},{"type":"p","text":"**Precautions**: friction-compensate so no external force acts; choose strips just before and just after the collision; repeat and average. **Sources of error**: friction not fully balanced, tape drag, reading dot spacings."},{"type":"h","text":"Method for every momentum problem"},{"type":"steps","items":["Draw BEFORE and AFTER sketches with masses and velocities. Mark the + direction.","Write: total momentum before = total momentum after.","Put in the numbers, remembering a minus sign for anything moving in the − direction.","Solve for the unknown. A negative answer means the body moves in the − direction."]},{"type":"warn","text":"Forgetting the minus sign for a body moving the opposite way is the No. 1 error in momentum questions. Also: in a recoil question the total momentum BEFORE is ZERO, not \"the bullet's momentum\"."}],"examples":[{"id":"week03.3.5.e1","title":"Collision — bodies stick together","question":"A 2.0 kg trolley moving at 3.0 m s^−1^ hits a stationary 1.0 kg trolley. They stick together. Find their velocity just after the collision.","img":{"src":"img/week03/collision","alt":"A 2.0 kg trolley moving at 3.0 m s−1 hits a stationary 1.0 kg trolley. They stick together. Find their velocity just after the collision.","w":1214,"h":480},"steps":[{"label":"Principle","text":"total momentum before = total momentum after"},{"label":"Before","text":"⟪(2.0\\times 3.0)+(1.0\\times 0)=6.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫"},{"label":"After","text":"⟪(2.0+1.0)\\times v=3.0v⟫"},{"label":"Solve","text":"⟪3.0v=6.0⟫, so ⟪v=6.0/3.0⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪v=\\boldsymbol{2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}}⟫ in the original direction"}],"answer":"2.0 m s^−1^"},{"id":"week03.3.5.e2","title":"Recoil (explosion from rest)","question":"A rifle of mass 4.0 kg fires a bullet of mass 0.010 kg at 400 m s^−1^. Calculate the recoil velocity of the rifle.","img":{"src":"img/week03/recoil","alt":"A rifle of mass 4.0 kg fires a bullet of mass 0.010 kg at 400 m s−1. Calculate the recoil velocity of the rifle.","w":1174,"h":288},"steps":[{"label":"Before","text":"everything at rest → total momentum ⟪=0⟫"},{"label":"After","text":"⟪(0.010\\times 400)+(4.0\\times v)=4.0+4.0v⟫"},{"label":"Conservation","text":"⟪0=4.0+4.0v⟫, so ⟪4.0v=-4.0⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪v=\\boldsymbol{-1.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}}⟫: the rifle recoils at **1.0 m s^−1^ backwards** (opposite to the bullet)"}],"answer":"1.0 m s^−1^ backwards"},{"id":"week03.3.5.e3","title":"Collision — bodies bounce apart","question":"Ball A (0.50 kg) moving at 4.0 m s^−1^ to the right hits ball B (1.5 kg) at rest. After the collision A bounces back at 2.0 m s^−1^ to the left. Find the velocity of B.","steps":[{"label":"Sign convention","text":"right = +, left = −. So A's velocity after ⟪=-2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫"},{"label":"Before","text":"⟪(0.50\\times 4.0)+(1.5\\times 0)=2.0\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫"},{"label":"After","text":"⟪(0.50\\times -2.0)+1.5v_{B}=-1.0+1.5v_{B}⟫"},{"label":"Conservation","text":"⟪2.0=-1.0+1.5v_{B}⟫, so ⟪1.5v_{B}=3.0⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪v_{B}=\\boldsymbol{2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}}⟫** to the right**"}],"answer":"2.0 m s^−1^ to the right"},{"id":"week03.3.5.e4","title":"Force from change of momentum","question":"A footballer kicks a 0.40 kg ball from rest to 25 m s^−1^. His boot is in contact with the ball for 0.050 s. Calculate the average force on the ball.","steps":[{"label":"Change in momentum","text":"⟪\\Delta p=mv-mu=(0.40\\times 25)-0=10\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫"},{"label":"Formula","text":"⟪F=\\Delta p/t⟫"},{"label":"Substitute","text":"⟪F=10/0.050⟫"},{"label":"Answer","text":"⟪F=\\boldsymbol{200\\,\\mathrm{N}}⟫"}],"answer":"200 N"}]},"tryit":[{"id":"week03.3.5.p1","kind":"self","q":"Define **linear momentum** and state its SI unit.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Momentum = mass × velocity (1); unit kg m s⁻¹ (or N s) (1).","points":[{"text":"Momentum = mass × velocity","marks":1},{"text":"unit kg m s⁻¹ (or N s)","marks":1}],"section":"try"},{"id":"week03.3.5.p2","kind":"num","q":"Calculate the momentum of a 1500 kg car travelling at 20 m s^−1^.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪p=mv=1500\\times 20⟫ (1) ⟪=30\\,000\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪p=mv=1500\\times 20⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=30\\,000\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":30000,"sf":1,"unit":"kg m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"section":"try","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 4 (Force from change of momentum). Start like this: Change in momentum: ⟪\\Delta p=mv-mu=(0.40\\times 25)-0=10\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","from":4}}],"practice":[{"id":"week03.3.5.p3","kind":"self","q":"State the principle of conservation of linear momentum.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"In a closed/isolated system with no external (resultant) force (1), the total momentum before a collision/explosion equals the total momentum after (1).","points":[{"text":"In a closed/isolated system with no external (resultant) force","marks":1},{"text":"the total momentum before a collision/explosion equals the total momentum after","marks":1}],"section":"practice"},{"id":"week03.3.5.p4","kind":"num","q":"A car of mass 1000 kg moving at 20 m s^−1^ crashes into the back of a stationary car of mass 1500 kg. They lock together. Find their common velocity just after the crash.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Momentum before ⟪=1000\\times 20=20\\,000\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1); after ⟪=(1000+1500)v=2500v⟫ (1); ⟪v=20\\,000\\div 2500=8.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Momentum before ⟪=1000\\times 20=20\\,000\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"after ⟪=(1000+1500)v=2500v⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪v=20\\,000\\div 2500=8.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":8,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"conf":"low","section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Collision — bodies stick together). Start like this: Principle: total momentum before = total momentum after","from":1}},{"id":"week03.3.5.p5","kind":"self","q":"A 3.0 kg trolley moving at 4.0 m s^−1^ to the right collides head-on with a 2.0 kg trolley moving at 1.0 m s^−1^ to the left. They stick together. Find the velocity after the collision.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Right = +. Before: ⟪(3.0\\times 4.0)+(2.0\\times -1.0)=12-2.0=10\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1); after: 5.0v (1); ⟪v=10\\div 5.0=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ to the right (1).","points":[{"text":"Right = +. Before: ⟪(3.0\\times 4.0)+(2.0\\times -1.0)=12-2.0=10\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"after: 5.0v","marks":1},{"text":"⟪v=10\\div 5.0=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ to the right","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 1 (Collision — bodies stick together). Start like this: Principle: total momentum before = total momentum after","from":1}},{"id":"week03.3.5.p6","kind":"self","q":"Two ice-skaters stand at rest facing each other and push apart. The 40 kg skater moves off at 3.0 m s^−1^. Find the velocity of the 60 kg skater.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Total momentum before ⟪=0⟫ (1); ⟪0=(40\\times 3.0)+60v⟫ (1); ⟪v=-120\\div 60=-2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫, i.e. 2.0 m s⁻¹ in the opposite direction (1).","points":[{"text":"Total momentum before ⟪=0⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪0=(40\\times 3.0)+60v⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪v=-120\\div 60=-2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫, i.e. 2.0 m s⁻¹ in the opposite direction","marks":1}],"section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 3 (Collision — bodies bounce apart). Start like this: Sign convention: right = +, left = −. So A's velocity after ⟪=-2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","from":3}},{"id":"week03.3.5.p7","kind":"num","q":"A cannon of mass 500 kg fires a 5.0 kg shell at 200 m s^−1^. Calculate the recoil speed of the cannon.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪0=(5.0\\times 200)+500v⟫ (1); ⟪500v=-1000⟫ (1); recoil speed ⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (backwards) (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪0=(5.0\\times 200)+500v⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪500v=-1000⟫","marks":1},{"text":"recoil speed ⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (backwards)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":2,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"conf":"low","section":"practice","hint":{"text":"Look back at Example 2 (Recoil (explosion from rest)). Start like this: Before: everything at rest → total momentum ⟪=0⟫","from":2}},{"id":"week03.3.5.p8","kind":"self","q":"Explain, using momentum, how a rocket can accelerate in space where there is no air to push against.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"The rocket burns fuel and pushes hot gases out backwards at high speed (1); the gases gain momentum backwards (1); by conservation of momentum (or Newton's 3rd law) the rocket gains an equal momentum forwards, so it accelerates (1).","points":[{"text":"The rocket burns fuel and pushes hot gases out backwards at high speed","marks":1},{"text":"the gases gain momentum backwards","marks":1},{"text":"by conservation of momentum (or Newton's 3rd law) the rocket gains an equal momentum forwards, so it accelerates","marks":1}],"section":"practice"}],"exit":[{"id":"week03.3.5.p9","kind":"self","q":"Explain how an air bag reduces injury to a driver in a crash.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"The driver's change in momentum is the same with or without the bag (1). The air bag increases the time taken for the driver to stop (1). Since ⟪F=\\Delta p/t⟫, a longer time gives a smaller force on the driver (1). (Also accept: spreads force over larger area → less pressure.)","points":[{"text":"The driver's change in momentum is the same with or without the bag","marks":1},{"text":"The air bag increases the time taken for the driver to stop","marks":1},{"text":"Since ⟪F=\\Delta p/t⟫, a longer time gives a smaller force on the driver (Also accept: spreads force over larger area → less pressure.)","marks":1}],"section":"exit"},{"id":"week03.3.5.p10","kind":"self","q":"A 0.20 kg ball hits a wall at 10 m s^−1^ and bounces straight back at 8.0 m s^−1^. It is in contact with the wall for 0.040 s. Calculate (a) the change in momentum, (b) the average force on the ball.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":5,"scheme":"(a) Taking the original direction +: ⟪\\Delta p=mv-mu=0.20\\times (-8.0)-0.20\\times 10⟫ (1) ⟪=-1.6-2.0=-3.6\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (size 3.6 kg m s⁻¹) (1).\n(b) ⟪F=\\Delta p/t=3.6\\div 0.040⟫ (1) ⟪=90\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (directed away from the wall) (1).","points":[{"text":"(a) Taking the original direction +: ⟪\\Delta p=mv-mu=0.20\\times (-8.0)-0.20\\times 10⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=-1.6-2.0=-3.6\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (size 3.6 kg m s⁻¹)","marks":1},{"text":"(b) ⟪F=\\Delta p/t=3.6\\div 0.040⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=90\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (directed away from the wall)","marks":1}],"section":"exit"}],"warmup":[{"id":"week03.3.5.w1","kind":"self","q":"Calculate the resultant force needed to give a 2.5 kg mass an acceleration of 4.0 m s^−2^.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪F=ma=2.5\\times 4.0⟫ (1) ⟪=10\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪F=ma=2.5\\times 4.0⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=10\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week03.3.4.p2"},{"id":"week03.3.5.w2","kind":"self","q":"A car of mass 1000 kg travels at a constant velocity of 25 m s^−1^ with a driving force of 2000 N. State the total resistive force and explain your answer.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"2000 N (1). Constant velocity → acceleration zero → resultant force zero, so forces are balanced (Newton's 1st law) (1).","points":[{"text":"2000 N","marks":1},{"text":"Constant velocity → acceleration zero → resultant force zero, so forces are balanced (Newton's 1st law)","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week03.3.4.p5"},{"id":"week03.3.5.w3","kind":"self","q":"Write down the equation for Newton's second law and give the unit of each quantity.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪F=ma⟫ (1); F in newtons (N), m in kilograms (kg), a in m s⁻² (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪F=ma⟫","marks":1},{"text":"F in newtons (N), m in kilograms (kg), a in m s⁻²","marks":1}],"section":"warmup","src":"week03.3.4.p1"}],"n":19},{"id":"week03.check","kind":"check","title":"Book check","titleText":"Book check","check":true,"watchOut":["Saying a horizontal line on a velocity–time graph means \"stopped\". It means CONSTANT VELOCITY (on a displacement–time graph it means at rest).","Using the total of all forces in ⟪F=ma⟫ instead of the RESULTANT force (subtract forces acting in opposite directions first).","Calculating average speed as the average of the speeds. Average speed = TOTAL distance ÷ TOTAL time.","Forgetting to convert km h^−1^ to ⟪\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}\\ (\\div 3.6)⟫, cm to m, or g to kg before substituting.","Giving acceleration in m s^−1^ instead of m s^−2^, or momentum in N instead of kg m s^−1^ (N s).","In momentum problems, forgetting the minus sign for a body moving in the opposite direction.","In recoil/explosion questions, forgetting that total momentum before is ZERO.","Naming weight and the normal reaction as a Newton's third law pair (they act on the SAME body).","Defining terms loosely (\"acceleration is going faster\"). Use \"rate of change of velocity\".","Dividing by only one mass in a \"stick together\" collision — divide by the TOTAL mass."],"mcq":[{"id":"week03.check.m1","kind":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is a VECTOR quantity?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["Speed","Distance","Velocity","Time"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Velocity has size and direction. Speed (A) is the most tempting distractor — it is the SIZE of velocity only, so it is a scalar; distance and time are also scalars.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week03.check.m2","kind":"mcq","q":"The gradient of a velocity–time graph gives","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["displacement","acceleration","speed","momentum"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Gradient ⟪=\\Delta v/\\Delta t⟫ = acceleration. Displacement (A) is the AREA under a v–t graph, not its gradient.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week03.check.m3","kind":"mcq","q":"The area under a velocity–time graph represents","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["acceleration","average velocity","distance travelled","force"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Area = velocity × time = distance (displacement). Acceleration (A) is the gradient.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week03.check.m4","kind":"mcq","q":"On a displacement–time graph, a horizontal straight line shows that the object is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["at rest","moving at constant velocity","accelerating","decelerating"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Displacement is not changing, so velocity (gradient) ⟪=0⟫: at rest. B is the meaning of a horizontal line on a VELOCITY–time graph — a common mix-up.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week03.check.m5","kind":"mcq","q":"A speed of 72 km h^−1^ is equal to","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["7.2 m s^−1^","20 m s^−1^","72 m s^−1^","259 m s^−1^"],"answer":"B","explanation":"⟪72\\div 3.6=20\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫. D (259) comes from multiplying by 3.6 instead of dividing.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week03.check.m6","kind":"mcq","q":"A car accelerates uniformly from rest to 30 m s^−1^ in 6.0 s. Its acceleration is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["0.20 m s^−2^","5.0 m s^−2^","24 m s^−2^","180 m s^−2^"],"answer":"B","explanation":"⟪a=(30-0)/6.0=5.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫. D (180) comes from multiplying ⟪v\\times t⟫ (that is not a formula for acceleration).","section":"p01"},{"id":"week03.check.m7","kind":"mcq","q":"Aristotle believed that","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["all objects fall with the same acceleration","a moving object keeps moving unless a force stops it","a force is needed to keep an object moving","forces always occur in equal and opposite pairs"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Aristotle thought a constant force was needed to maintain motion. A and B are what Galileo and Newton showed, which is why they are tempting but wrong.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week03.check.m8","kind":"mcq","q":"A passenger in a bus is thrown forward when the bus stops suddenly. This is best explained by","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["Newton's first law","Newton's third law","conservation of energy","the principle of moments"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Inertia (1st law): the passenger keeps moving forward because no force stops him. The 3rd law (B) is about pairs of forces on two different bodies and does not explain this.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week03.check.m9","kind":"mcq","q":"A book rests on a table. The Newton's third law partner of the book's weight is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["the upward push of the table on the book","the downward push of the book on the table","the upward gravitational pull of the book on the Earth","the friction between the book and the table"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Weight = Earth pulls book down, so its partner = book pulls Earth up (same type, different body). A is equal and opposite but acts on the SAME body (the book), so it is not a third-law pair.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week03.check.m10","kind":"mcq","q":"Forces of 15 N to the right and 5 N to the left act on a 2.0 kg block. Its acceleration is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["5.0 m s^−2^ to the right","10 m s^−2^ to the right","7.5 m s^−2^ to the right","20 m s^−2^ to the right"],"answer":"A","explanation":"Resultant ⟪=15-5=10\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ right; ⟪a=10/2.0=5.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫. B (10 m s⁻²) comes from ADDING the forces ⟪(20\\div 2.0)⟫: always subtract forces in opposite directions.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week03.check.m11","kind":"mcq","q":"A skydiver is falling at terminal velocity. Which statement is correct?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["Her weight is greater than the air resistance","The air resistance is greater than her weight","The air resistance equals her weight","There are no forces acting on her"],"answer":"C","explanation":"Terminal velocity: constant velocity, so resultant ⟪=0⟫ and drag = weight. D is wrong: forces still act, they are just balanced.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week03.check.m12","kind":"mcq","q":"Which is a correct unit of momentum?","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["kg m s^−2^","N s","N m","kg m^2^ s^−1^"],"answer":"B","explanation":"⟪p=mv\\rightarrow \\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫, which equals N s (since ⟪N=\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫). A (kg m s⁻²) is the newton — the unit of FORCE.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week03.check.m13","kind":"mcq","q":"A 1.0 kg trolley moving at 6.0 m s^−1^ hits a stationary 2.0 kg trolley and they stick together. Their common velocity is","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["2.0 m s^−1^","3.0 m s^−1^","6.0 m s^−1^","12 m s^−1^"],"answer":"A","explanation":"⟪1.0\\times 6.0=6.0=(1.0+2.0)v\\rightarrow v=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫. B (3.0) comes from dividing by the 2.0 kg mass only instead of the TOTAL mass.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week03.check.m14","kind":"mcq","q":"A rifle recoils when a bullet is fired because","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["the bullet's kinetic energy is conserved","the total momentum of rifle and bullet stays zero","the rifle is heavier than the bullet","air pushes back on the rifle"],"answer":"B","explanation":"Before firing the total momentum is zero; afterwards the bullet's forward momentum must be balanced by equal backward momentum of the rifle. C explains why the recoil is SLOW, not why it happens.","section":"p01"},{"id":"week03.check.m15","kind":"mcq","q":"Crumple zones make cars safer in a collision because they","marks":1,"level":1,"options":["reduce the change in momentum","increase the mass of the car","increase the time taken to stop, reducing the force","make the car stop more quickly"],"answer":"C","explanation":"⟪F=\\Delta p/t⟫: the same Δp over a longer time gives a smaller force. A is wrong — the change in momentum is the same; only the TIME changes.","section":"p01"}],"structured":[{"id":"week03.check.s1","kind":"multi","title":"A cyclist's journey (motion graphs)","total":12,"stem":["A cyclist starts from rest and accelerates uniformly to 8.0 m s^−1^ in 4.0 s. She then rides at this constant velocity for 10 s, and finally decelerates uniformly to rest in 2.0 s."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week03.check.s1.a","kind":"self","q":"Define **acceleration**.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"The rate of change of velocity / change in velocity per unit time (1).","points":[{"text":"The rate of change of velocity / change in velocity per unit time","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week03.check.s1.b","kind":"self","q":"Sketch a velocity–time graph of the whole journey. Label the axes and show the values of velocity and time at each change.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":9,"scheme":"Axes labelled velocity / m s⁻¹ and time / s (1); straight line from (0, 0) to (4, 8.0), then horizontal to (14, 8.0) (1); straight line down to (16, 0) (1).","points":[{"text":"Axes labelled velocity / m s⁻¹ and time / s","marks":1},{"text":"straight line from (0, 0) to (4, 8.0), then horizontal to (14, 8.0)","marks":1},{"text":"straight line down to (16, 0)","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week03.check.s1.c","kind":"self","q":"Calculate the acceleration during the first 4.0 s.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪a=(8.0-0)/4.0⟫ (1) ⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪a=(8.0-0)/4.0⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":2,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−2^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}"},"label":"(c)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week03.check.s1.d","kind":"self","q":"Calculate the total distance travelled.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 4.0\\times 8.0=16\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ and ⟪10\\times 8.0=80\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1); ⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 2.0\\times 8.0=8.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1); total ⟪=104\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 4.0\\times 8.0=16\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫ and ⟪10\\times 8.0=80\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times 2.0\\times 8.0=8.0\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"total ⟪=104\\,\\mathrm{m}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":104,"sf":3,"unit":"m","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}"},"conf":"low","label":"(d)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week03.check.s1.e","kind":"self","q":"Calculate the average speed for the whole journey.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"total time ⟪=16\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫ (1); average speed ⟪=104\\div 16=6.5\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"total time ⟪=16\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"average speed ⟪=104\\div 16=6.5\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":6.5,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"label":"(e)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week03.check.s1.f","kind":"self","q":"The cyclist rode in a straight line. State why her displacement has the same size as the distance travelled.","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":2,"scheme":"She moved in one direction in a straight line without turning back, so the straight-line distance from start to finish equals the path length (1).","points":[{"text":"She moved in one direction in a straight line without turning back, so the straight-line distance from start to finish equals the path length","marks":1}],"label":"(f)"}]},{"id":"week03.check.s2","kind":"multi","title":"Newton's laws and a car","total":13,"stem":["A car of mass 800 kg moves along a straight, level road."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week03.check.s2.a","kind":"self","q":"State Newton's first law of motion.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"A body stays at rest or moves with uniform velocity in a straight line (1) unless acted on by a resultant/unbalanced force (1).","points":[{"text":"A body stays at rest or moves with uniform velocity in a straight line","marks":1},{"text":"unless acted on by a resultant/unbalanced force","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week03.check.s2.b","kind":"self","q":"Aristotle would have said that the engine must keep pushing just to keep the car moving at a steady speed. Explain how Newton's view differs.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Newton: no resultant force is needed to keep a body moving at constant velocity (1); the engine force is only needed to balance friction/air resistance — the forces are balanced (1).","points":[{"text":"Newton: no resultant force is needed to keep a body moving at constant velocity","marks":1},{"text":"the engine force is only needed to balance friction/air resistance — the forces are balanced","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week03.check.s2.c","kind":"self","q":"The driving force is 2400 N and the total resistive force is 800 N. Calculate the acceleration of the car.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Resultant ⟪=2400-800=1600\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (1); ⟪a=F/m=1600\\div 800⟫ (1) ⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Resultant ⟪=2400-800=1600\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪a=F/m=1600\\div 800⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":2,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−2^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}"},"label":"(c)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week03.check.s2.d","kind":"self","q":"As the car speeds up, the air resistance increases. Explain why the car eventually reaches a maximum steady speed.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Air resistance increases with speed until total resistive force equals the driving force (1); the resultant force is then zero so acceleration is zero — constant (maximum) speed (1).","points":[{"text":"Air resistance increases with speed until total resistive force equals the driving force","marks":1},{"text":"the resultant force is then zero so acceleration is zero — constant (maximum) speed","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week03.check.s2.e","kind":"self","q":"State Newton's third law and use it to explain how the tyres make the car move forward.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"If A exerts a force on B, B exerts an equal and opposite force on A (1). The tyres push the road backwards, so the road pushes the tyres (car) forwards (1).","points":[{"text":"If A exerts a force on B, B exerts an equal and opposite force on A","marks":1},{"text":"The tyres push the road backwards, so the road pushes the tyres (car) forwards","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"},{"id":"week03.check.s2.f","kind":"self","q":"The car brakes from 20 m s^−1^ to rest in 5.0 s. Calculate the braking force.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪a=(0-20)/5.0=-4.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫ (1); ⟪F=ma=800\\times 4.0=3200\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (backwards) (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪a=(0-20)/5.0=-4.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-2}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪F=ma=800\\times 4.0=3200\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (backwards)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":3200,"sf":2,"unit":"N","unitTex":"\\mathrm{N}"},"conf":"low","label":"(f)","autoKind":"num"}]},{"id":"week03.check.s3","kind":"multi","title":"Railway trucks (momentum)","total":12,"stem":["A railway truck of mass 4000 kg moving at 3.0 m s^−1^ collides with a stationary truck of mass 2000 kg on a level track. The two trucks couple (join) together."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week03.check.s3.a","kind":"self","q":"Define **linear momentum** and state its unit.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"Mass × velocity (1); kg m s⁻¹ or N s (1).","points":[{"text":"Mass × velocity","marks":1},{"text":"kg m s⁻¹ or N s","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week03.check.s3.b","kind":"self","q":"State the principle of conservation of linear momentum.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"For a closed system with no external force (1), total momentum before a collision = total momentum after (1).","points":[{"text":"For a closed system with no external force","marks":1},{"text":"total momentum before a collision = total momentum after","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week03.check.s3.c","kind":"self","q":"Calculate the velocity of the coupled trucks just after the collision.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Before: ⟪4000\\times 3.0=12\\,000\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1); after: ⟪(4000+2000)v=6000v⟫ (1); ⟪v=12\\,000\\div 6000=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Before: ⟪4000\\times 3.0=12\\,000\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"after: ⟪(4000+2000)v=6000v⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪v=12\\,000\\div 6000=2.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":2,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"conf":"low","label":"(c)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week03.check.s3.d","kind":"self","q":"The coupling takes 0.50 s. Calculate the average force exerted on the 2000 kg truck.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Δp of 2000 kg truck ⟪=2000\\times 2.0-0=4000\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1); ⟪F=\\Delta p/t=4000\\div 0.50=8000\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Δp of 2000 kg truck ⟪=2000\\times 2.0-0=4000\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪F=\\Delta p/t=4000\\div 0.50=8000\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":8000,"sf":1,"unit":"N","unitTex":"\\mathrm{N}"},"label":"(d)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week03.check.s3.e","kind":"self","q":"Describe briefly how you could verify the principle of conservation of momentum in the laboratory using two trolleys.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":6,"scheme":"Friction-compensated runway; measure masses of both trolleys (1); push trolley A (with pin) into stationary B (with cork) so they stick; use ticker tape/light gates to find velocity before and after (1); calculate mAuA and ⟪(\\mathrm{mA}+mB)v⟫ and show they are equal (1).","points":[{"text":"Friction-compensated runway; measure masses of both trolleys","marks":1},{"text":"push trolley A (with pin) into stationary B (with cork) so they stick; use ticker tape/light gates to find velocity before and after","marks":1},{"text":"calculate mAuA and ⟪(\\mathrm{mA}+mB)v⟫ and show they are equal","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"}]}],"n":20},{"id":"week03.past","kind":"past","title":"Past-paper practice","titleText":"Past-paper practice","check":true,"log":true,"questions":[{"id":"week03.past.q1","kind":"multi","title":"Data analysis (Paper 02 Question 1 style)","total":25,"stem":["A student released a trolley of mass 0.80 kg down a ramp. Using light gates and a data-logger, she measured the velocity, v, of the trolley at different times, t. Her results are shown below."],"stemBlocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Time, t / s","0","0.5","1.0","1.5","2.0","2.5","3.0"],"rows":[["Velocity, v / m s^−1^","0.40","0.92","1.38","1.93","2.41","2.88","3.42"]]}],"parts":[{"id":"week03.past.q1.a","kind":"self","q":"On the grid provided, plot a graph of velocity, v (y-axis), against time, t (x-axis). Draw the best-fit line.","marks":7,"level":3,"img":{"src":"img/week03/graph_paper","alt":"On the grid provided, plot a graph of velocity, v (y-axis), against time, t (x-axis). Draw the best-fit line.","w":1174,"h":894},"lines":0,"scheme":"Axes labelled with quantity and unit ⟪(1+1)⟫; suitable linear scale using more than half the grid (1); all 7 points plotted correctly (2: −1 per error, max 2); single straight best-fit line (1); title (1).","points":[{"text":"Axes labelled with quantity and unit","marks":2},{"text":"suitable linear scale using more than half the grid","marks":1},{"text":"all 7 points plotted correctly","marks":2},{"text":"single straight best-fit line","marks":1},{"text":"title","marks":1}],"label":"(a)","grid":true},{"id":"week03.past.q1.b","kind":"self","q":"Determine the gradient of your graph. Show clearly the triangle used.","marks":4,"level":3,"lines":4,"scheme":"Large triangle drawn on the line (1); correct Δv and Δt read from the line (1); gradient ≈ 1.0 (accept 0.95–1.05) (1); unit m s⁻² (1).","points":[{"text":"Large triangle drawn on the line","marks":1},{"text":"correct Δv and Δt read from the line","marks":1},{"text":"gradient ≈ 1.0 (accept 0.95–1.05)","marks":1},{"text":"unit m s⁻²","marks":1}],"label":"(b)"},{"id":"week03.past.q1.c","kind":"self","q":"What physical quantity does the gradient represent?","marks":1,"level":1,"lines":1,"scheme":"The acceleration of the trolley (1).","points":[{"text":"The acceleration of the trolley","marks":1}],"label":"(c)"},{"id":"week03.past.q1.d","kind":"self","q":"State the value of the intercept on the v-axis and explain what it tells you about the trolley at ⟪t=0⟫.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Intercept ≈ 0.40 m s⁻¹ (accept 0.35–0.45) (1); the trolley was already moving at ⟪t=0⟫ / had an initial velocity (it was not released from rest at the start of timing) (1).","points":[{"text":"Intercept ≈ 0.40 m s⁻¹ (accept 0.35–0.45)","marks":1},{"text":"the trolley was already moving at ⟪t=0⟫ / had an initial velocity (it was not released from rest at the start of timing)","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week03.past.q1.e","kind":"self","q":"Use your graph to find the distance travelled by the trolley between ⟪t=0⟫ and ⟪t=3.0\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Distance = area under the graph (1); trapezium ⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times (0.40+3.40)\\times 3.0⟫ (values read from line) (1); ≈ 5.7 m (accept 5.5–5.9 m) (1).","points":[{"text":"Distance = area under the graph","marks":1},{"text":"trapezium ⟪\\tfrac{1}{2}\\times (0.40+3.40)\\times 3.0⟫ (values read from line)","marks":1},{"text":"≈ 5.7 m (accept 5.5–5.9 m)","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"},{"id":"week03.past.q1.f","kind":"self","q":"Calculate the resultant force on the trolley.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"⟪F=ma=0.80\\times 1.0⟫ (1) = 0.80 N (ecf from gradient) (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪F=ma=0.80\\times 1.0⟫","marks":1},{"text":"= 0.80 N (ecf from gradient)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":0.8,"sf":2,"unit":"N","unitTex":"\\mathrm{N}"},"conf":"low","label":"(f)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week03.past.q1.g","kind":"self","q":"Calculate the momentum of the trolley at ⟪t=3.0\\,\\mathrm{s}⟫.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":2,"scheme":"⟪p=mv=0.80\\times 3.4⟫ (1) ⟪=2.7\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (accept 2.7–2.8) (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪p=mv=0.80\\times 3.4⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=2.7\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (accept 2.7–2.8)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":2.7,"sf":2,"unit":"kg m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}","min":2.7,"max":2.8},"label":"(g)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week03.past.q1.h","kind":"self","q":"Describe the motion of the trolley, giving a reason from the graph.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Uniform (constant) acceleration (1); the graph is a straight line with constant positive gradient (1).","points":[{"text":"Uniform (constant) acceleration","marks":1},{"text":"the graph is a straight line with constant positive gradient","marks":1}],"label":"(h)"},{"id":"week03.past.q1.i","kind":"self","q":"Suggest ONE other method the student could use to measure the velocity and ONE precaution to improve the accuracy of her results.","marks":2,"level":2,"lines":3,"scheme":"Ticker-timer and tape / stopwatch with marked distances (1); repeat each reading and average / friction-compensate / ensure the ramp angle is fixed / release from the same point each time (1).","points":[{"text":"Ticker-timer and tape / stopwatch with marked distances","marks":1},{"text":"repeat each reading and average / friction-compensate / ensure the ramp angle is fixed / release from the same point each time","marks":1}],"label":"(i)"}]},{"id":"week03.past.q2","kind":"multi","title":"Road safety (extended response style)","total":15,"stem":["A car of mass 1200 kg travelling at 15 m s^−1^ crashes into the back of a stationary car of mass 800 kg. The two cars lock together."],"stemBlocks":[],"parts":[{"id":"week03.past.q2.a","kind":"self","q":"State Newton's first law of motion and use it to explain why a passenger who is not wearing a seat belt is thrown forward in the crash.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":5,"scheme":"A body continues at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line unless a resultant force acts (1). The car is stopped by the collision force, but no force acts on the passenger (1), so the passenger continues moving forward (inertia) and hits the dashboard/windscreen (1).","points":[{"text":"A body continues at rest or in uniform motion in a straight line unless a resultant force acts","marks":1},{"text":"The car is stopped by the collision force, but no force acts on the passenger","marks":1},{"text":"so the passenger continues moving forward (inertia) and hits the dashboard/windscreen","marks":1}],"label":"(a)"},{"id":"week03.past.q2.b","kind":"self","q":"Calculate the velocity of the two cars just after the collision.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"Momentum before ⟪=1200\\times 15=18\\,000\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1); after ⟪=(1200+800)v=2000v⟫ (1); ⟪v=18\\,000\\div 2000=9.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1).","points":[{"text":"Momentum before ⟪=1200\\times 15=18\\,000\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"after ⟪=(1200+800)v=2000v⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪v=18\\,000\\div 2000=9.0\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1}],"num":{"value":9,"sf":2,"unit":"m s^−1^","unitTex":"\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}"},"conf":"low","label":"(b)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week03.past.q2.c","kind":"self","q":"The collision lasts 0.30 s. Calculate the average force on the 1200 kg car.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":4,"scheme":"⟪\\Delta p=1200\\times (9.0-15)=-7200\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫ (1); ⟪F=\\Delta p/t=7200\\div 0.30⟫ (1) ⟪=24\\,000\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (backwards) (1).","points":[{"text":"⟪\\Delta p=1200\\times (9.0-15)=-7200\\,\\mathrm{kg}\\,\\mathrm{m}\\,\\mathrm{s}^{-1}⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪F=\\Delta p/t=7200\\div 0.30⟫","marks":1},{"text":"⟪=24\\,000\\,\\mathrm{N}⟫ (backwards)","marks":1}],"num":{"value":24000,"sf":2,"unit":"N","unitTex":"\\mathrm{N}"},"conf":"low","label":"(c)","autoKind":"num"},{"id":"week03.past.q2.d","kind":"self","q":"Modern cars have crumple zones. Use the idea of momentum to explain how a crumple zone reduces the force on the people in the car.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":5,"scheme":"The change in momentum of the passengers is fixed by the crash (1). The crumple zone crushes slowly so the collision lasts longer (1). Since ⟪F=\\Delta p/t⟫, a longer time gives a smaller force (1).","points":[{"text":"The change in momentum of the passengers is fixed by the crash","marks":1},{"text":"The crumple zone crushes slowly so the collision lasts longer","marks":1},{"text":"Since ⟪F=\\Delta p/t⟫, a longer time gives a smaller force","marks":1}],"label":"(d)"},{"id":"week03.past.q2.e","kind":"self","q":"After the collision the locked cars slide and come to rest. An Aristotelian thinker would say they stop because rest is their \"natural state\". Using Newton's laws, give the correct explanation.","marks":3,"level":2,"lines":5,"scheme":"Moving bodies do not stop by themselves — without a resultant force they would keep moving (1st law) (1). Friction between the tyres and road (and air resistance) provides a resultant force opposite to the motion (1), which causes a deceleration ⟪(F=ma)⟫ until the cars stop (1).","points":[{"text":"Moving bodies do not stop by themselves — without a resultant force they would keep moving (1st law)","marks":1},{"text":"Friction between the tyres and road (and air resistance) provides a resultant force opposite to the motion","marks":1},{"text":"which causes a deceleration ⟪(F=ma)⟫ until the cars stop","marks":1}],"label":"(e)"}]}],"n":21}]};
